1
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,490
Drink up, me hearties, yo ho

2
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:38,160
We kidnap and ravage and don't give a hoot

3
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,110
Drink up, me hearties, yo ho

4
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:45,610
Yo ho, yo ho

5
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:49,120
A pirate's life for me

6
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:53,520
We extort, we pilfer, we filch and sack

7
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:54,680
Drink up

8
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:56,520
Quiet, missy.

9
00:00:56,590 --> 00:00:59,180
Cursed pirates sail these waters.

10
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:00,950
You don't want to bring them.

11
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:03,660
Mr. Gibbs, that will do.

12
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:05,960
She was singing about pirates.

13
00:01:06,030 --> 00:01:07,760
It's bad luck to be singing about pirates

14
00:01:07,830 --> 00:01:09,890
with us mired in this unnatural fog.

15
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:11,130
Mark my words.

16
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,140
Consider them marked.

17
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,500
On your way.

18
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:16,670
Aye, Lieutenant.

19
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,340
It's bad luck to have a woman on board, too.

20
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:22,140
Even a miniature one.

21
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,450
I think it'd be rather exciting to meet a pirate.

22
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:28,540
Think again, Miss Swann.

23
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,350
Vile and dissolute creatures, the lot of them.

24
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:34,480
I intend that any man who sails under a pirate flag

25
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,430
or wears a pirate brand gets what he deserves.

26
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,430
A short drop and a sudden stop.

27
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:46,800
Lieutenant Norrington, I appreciate your fervor,

28
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:49,570
but I'm concerned about the effect

29
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,010
this subject will have upon my daughter.

30
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,940
My apologies, Governor Swann.

31
00:01:54,010 --> 00:01:56,040
Actually, I find it all fascinating.

32
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,740
Yes. That's what concerns me.

33
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:28,680
Look! A boy! There's a boy in the water!

34
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:33,120
Man overboard!

35
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:36,650
Man the ropes. Fetch a hook.

36
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:38,550
Haul him aboard.

37
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:48,800
He's still breathing.

38
00:02:48,870 --> 00:02:51,500
Mary, Mother of God.

39
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,370
What happened here?

40
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:01,850
It's the powder magazine.

41
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:04,310
Merchant vessels run heavily armed.

42
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:08,650
A lot of good it did them.

43
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:11,380
Everyone's thinking it. I'm just saying it.

44
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:14,050
Pirates.

45
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:17,590
There's no proof of that. It's probably an accident.

46
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,560
Rouse the captain immediately.

47
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:23,690
Heave to and take in sail. Launch the boats.

48
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:29,800
Elizabeth, I want you to accompany the boy.

49
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:31,940
He'll be in your charge.

50
00:03:32,010 --> 00:03:33,640
Take care of him.

51
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,020
It's okay.

52
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:57,870
My name's Elizabeth Swann.

53
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:00,460
Will Turner.

54
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:02,470
I'm watching over you, Will.

55
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:17,280
You're a pirate.

56
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:19,260
Has he said anything?

57
00:04:19,330 --> 00:04:23,490
His name's William Turner. That's all I found out.

58
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:25,690
Take him below.

59
00:05:35,130 --> 00:05:36,900
Elizabeth?

60
00:05:38,770 --> 00:05:41,370
Are you all right?

61
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:43,200
Are you decent?

62
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:44,570
Yes.

63
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:46,440
Yes.

64
00:05:46,510 --> 00:05:49,570
Still abed at this hour?

65
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:52,120
Oh, it's a beautiful day.

66
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:56,050
I have a gift for you.

67
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:00,560
Oh, it's beautiful.

68
00:06:00,630 --> 00:06:01,960
lsn't it?

69
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,460
May I inquire as to the occasion?

70
00:06:05,530 --> 00:06:08,900
Does a father need an occasion to dote upon his daughter?

71
00:06:10,170 --> 00:06:11,300
Go on.

72
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:16,170
Actually, I...

73
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,270
I hoped you might wear it for the ceremony today.

74
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:21,680
The ceremony?

75
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:24,550
Captain Norrington's promotion ceremony.

76
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:25,450
I knew it.

77
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:28,820
Commodore Norrington, as he's about to become.

78
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:31,620
A fine gentleman, don't you think?

79
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:33,960
He fancies you, you know.

80
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:37,000
Elizabeth? How's it coming?

81
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:39,500
It's difficult to say.

82
00:06:39,570 --> 00:06:41,970
I'm told it's the latest fashion in London.

83
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:45,940
Well, women in London must've learnt not to breathe.

84
00:06:47,370 --> 00:06:49,140
Milord, you have a visitor.

85
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:08,520
Mr. Turner.

86
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:10,190
Good to see you again.

87
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:11,850
Good day, sir.

88
00:07:12,870 --> 00:07:14,730
I have your order.

89
00:07:19,610 --> 00:07:20,660
Well.

90
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:23,000
The blade is folded steel.

91
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:26,270
That's gold filigree laid into the handle.

92
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:28,070
If I may.

93
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:31,350
Perfectly balanced.

94
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:35,110
The tang is nearly the full width of the blade.

95
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:39,660
lmpressive. Very impressive.

96
00:07:39,730 --> 00:07:41,320
Now, now.

97
00:07:41,390 --> 00:07:45,490
Commodore Norrington is going to be very pleased with this.

98
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:48,760
Do pass my compliments on to your master.

99
00:07:50,270 --> 00:07:52,360
I shall.

100
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:56,240
A craftsman is always pleased to hear his work is appreciated.

101
00:07:57,510 --> 00:08:01,340
Elizabeth, you look absolutely stunning.

102
00:08:01,410 --> 00:08:03,040
Will.

103
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:05,640
It's so good to see you.

104
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:08,020
I had a dream about you last night.

105
00:08:08,090 --> 00:08:08,880
About me?

106
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:11,120
Yes, is that entirely proper for you --

107
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:13,320
About the day we met. Do you remember?

108
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:15,380
How could I forget, Miss Swann?

109
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:18,490
How many times must I ask you to call me Elizabeth?

110
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:22,230
At least once more, Miss Swann, as always.

111
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:23,430
There. See?

112
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:25,870
At least the boy has a sense of propriety.

113
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:29,530
We really must be going. There you are.

114
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:31,600
Good day, Mr. Turner.

115
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:34,950
Come along.

116
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:38,340
Good day.

117
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:42,410
Elizabeth.

118
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:13,270
What -- hey.

119
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:16,210
Hold up, there, you.

120
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:20,340
It's a shilling to tie up your boat at the dock.

121
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:24,210
And I shall need to know your name.

122
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:28,490
What do you say to three shillings,

123
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:30,500
and we forget the name?

124
00:10:33,670 --> 00:10:36,400
Welcome to Port Royal, Mr. Smith.

125
00:11:11,970 --> 00:11:14,500
Two paces march!

126
00:11:16,310 --> 00:11:19,000
Right about-face!

127
00:11:21,350 --> 00:11:24,250
Present arms!

128
00:11:47,770 --> 00:11:50,440
This dock is off limits to civilians.

129
00:11:50,510 --> 00:11:52,310
I'm terribly sorry. I didn't know.

130
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:55,280
If I see one, I shall inform you immediately.

131
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:02,310
There's some sort of high-toned and fancy to-do up at the fort.

132
00:12:02,390 --> 00:12:05,650
How could it be two upstanding gentlemen such as yourselves

133
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:08,490
did not merit an invitation?

134
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:11,550
Someone has to make sure this dock stays off limits.

135
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:13,430
It's a fine goal, to be sure.

136
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:17,660
But it seems to me that a ship like that

137
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:21,070
makes this one here a bit superfluous, really.

138
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:24,010
The Dauntless is the power in these waters, true.

139
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:27,440
But there's no ship as can match the Interceptor for speed.

140
00:12:27,510 --> 00:12:28,610
I've heard of one.

141
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:31,950
It's supposed to be very fast. Nigh uncatchable.

142
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:33,780
The Black Pearl,

143
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:40,550
Well, there's no real ship as can match the Interceptor,

144
00:12:40,630 --> 00:12:42,590
Black Pearl is a real ship.

145
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:44,460
No. No, it's not.

146
00:12:44,530 --> 00:12:46,230
Yes, it is. I've seen it.

147
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:47,730
You've seen it?

148
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:48,660
Yes.

149
00:12:48,730 --> 00:12:50,700
You haven't seen it.

150
00:12:50,770 --> 00:12:51,960
Yes, I have.

151
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:55,270
You've seen a ship with black sails

152
00:12:55,340 --> 00:12:59,740
that's crewed by the damned and captained by a man so evil

153
00:12:59,810 --> 00:13:03,150
that Hell itself spat him back out?

154
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:04,410
No.

155
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:05,950
No.

156
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:08,350
But I have seen a ship with black sails.

157
00:13:08,420 --> 00:13:09,320
Oh.

158
00:13:09,390 --> 00:13:11,550
No ship that's not crewed by the damned

159
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:14,490
and captained by a man so evil that Hell spat him out

160
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:17,620
could possibly have black sails, therefore couldn't be

161
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:18,990
any other ship than the Black Pearl,

162
00:13:19,060 --> 00:13:21,830
Is that what you're saying?

163
00:13:21,900 --> 00:13:23,030
No.

164
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:25,970
Like I said, there's no real ship

165
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:28,530
as can match the Interceptor,

166
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,100
Hey!

167
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:33,580
You!

168
00:13:34,650 --> 00:13:35,770
Get away from there!

169
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:38,210
You don't have permission to be there.

170
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:40,550
I'm sorry. It's such a pretty boat.

171
00:13:40,620 --> 00:13:41,710
Ship.

172
00:13:41,790 --> 00:13:42,780
What's your name?

173
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:45,420
Smith. Or Smithy, if you like.

174
00:13:45,490 --> 00:13:48,860
What's your purpose in Port Royal, Mr. Smith?

175
00:13:48,930 --> 00:13:50,050
Yeah. And no lies.

176
00:13:50,130 --> 00:13:53,530
Well, then, I confess.

177
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:55,620
It is my intention to commandeer one of these ships,

178
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:57,600
pick up a crew in Tortuga, raid, pillage, plunder,

179
00:13:57,670 --> 00:14:00,430
and otherwise pilfer my weaselly black guts out.

180
00:14:00,510 --> 00:14:03,810
-I said no lies. -I think he's telling the truth.

181
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:07,540
If he were telling the truth, he wouldn't have told us.

182
00:14:07,610 --> 00:14:12,020
Unless you wouldn't believe the truth even if he told you.

183
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:24,120
May I have a moment?

184
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:34,700
You look lovely, Elizabeth.

185
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:42,640
I apologize if I seem forward, but I must speak my mind.

186
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:47,680
This promotion throws into sharp relief

187
00:14:47,750 --> 00:14:51,480
that which I have not yet achieved.

188
00:14:53,230 --> 00:14:55,690
A marriage to a fine woman.

189
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:59,690
You have become a fine woman, Elizabeth.

190
00:14:59,770 --> 00:15:01,700
I can't breathe.

191
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:05,670
Yes, I'm a bit nervous myself.

192
00:15:07,510 --> 00:15:09,770
And then they made me their chief.

193
00:15:12,180 --> 00:15:13,870
Elizabeth?

194
00:15:15,950 --> 00:15:16,880
Elizabeth!

195
00:15:16,950 --> 00:15:18,880
My God.

196
00:15:18,950 --> 00:15:19,680
The rocks!

197
00:15:19,750 --> 00:15:22,280
Sir, it's a miracle she missed them.

198
00:15:24,390 --> 00:15:27,290
-Will you be saving her? -I can't swim.

199
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:31,190
Pride of the King's Navy, you are.

200
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:33,160
Do not lose these.

201
00:15:47,310 --> 00:15:49,370
What was that?

202
00:16:22,850 --> 00:16:24,440
Make way!

203
00:16:31,590 --> 00:16:32,820
I got her!

204
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:35,050
She's not breathing!

205
00:16:35,130 --> 00:16:36,690
Move!

206
00:16:42,270 --> 00:16:44,700
I never would've thought of that.

207
00:16:44,770 --> 00:16:47,140
Clearly you've never been to Singapore.

208
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:54,840
Where did you get that?

209
00:16:56,110 --> 00:16:57,740
On your feet.

210
00:16:59,950 --> 00:17:01,150
Elizabeth!

211
00:17:02,450 --> 00:17:03,440
Are you all right?

212
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:04,890
Yes, I'm fine.

213
00:17:09,530 --> 00:17:10,290
Shoot him.

214
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:11,290
-Father. -What?

215
00:17:11,360 --> 00:17:14,860
Commodore, do you really intend to kill my rescuer?

216
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:23,870
I believe thanks are in order.

217
00:17:27,980 --> 00:17:32,110
Had a brush with the East lndia Trading Company, did we, pirate?

218
00:17:33,250 --> 00:17:34,620
Hang him.

219
00:17:34,690 --> 00:17:38,880
Keep your guns on him, men. Gillette, fetch some irons.

220
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:40,080
Well, well.

221
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:42,560
Jack Sparrow, isn't it?

222
00:17:42,630 --> 00:17:45,530
Captain Jack Sparrow, if you please, sir.

223
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:48,530
Well, I don't see your ship, Captain.

224
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:50,970
I'm in the market, as it were.

225
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:52,940
He said he'd come to commandeer one.

226
00:17:53,010 --> 00:17:56,630
Told you he was telling the truth. These are his, sir.

227
00:17:59,780 --> 00:18:02,470
No additional shot nor powder.

228
00:18:04,350 --> 00:18:06,870
A compass that doesn't point North.

229
00:18:11,090 --> 00:18:14,460
And I half expected it to be made of wood.

230
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:20,230
You are without doubt the worst pirate I've ever heard of.

231
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:22,390
But you have heard of me.

232
00:18:24,670 --> 00:18:27,140
Commodore, I really must protest.

233
00:18:27,210 --> 00:18:28,830
Carefully, Lieutenant.

234
00:18:28,910 --> 00:18:31,170
Pirate or not, this man saved my life.

235
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:34,740
One good deed is not enough to redeem a man

236
00:18:34,810 --> 00:18:37,040
Though it seems enough to condemn him.

237
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,520
lndeed.

238
00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:40,880
Finally.

239
00:18:41,390 --> 00:18:42,410
No.

240
00:18:42,490 --> 00:18:43,450
No! Don't shoot!

241
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:45,510
I knew you'd warm up to me.

242
00:18:45,590 --> 00:18:47,890
Commodore Norrington, my effects, please.

243
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:49,390
And my hat.

244
00:18:50,330 --> 00:18:51,660
Commodore.

245
00:18:54,230 --> 00:18:57,400
-lt is Elizabeth, isn't it? -It's Miss Swann.

246
00:18:57,470 --> 00:18:59,700
Miss Swann, if you'd be so kind.

247
00:18:59,770 --> 00:19:02,570
Come, come, dear. We don't have all day.

248
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:06,300
Now if you'll be very kind.

249
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:24,790
Easy on the goods, darling.

250
00:19:25,530 --> 00:19:26,930
You're despicable.

251
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,020
Sticks and stones, love.

252
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,700
I saved your life. You save mine. We're square.

253
00:19:32,770 --> 00:19:36,260
Gentlemen, milady, you will always remember this

254
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:41,400
as the day that you almost caught Captain Jack Sparrow.

255
00:19:58,000 --> 00:19:59,960
Whoa!

256
00:20:00,030 --> 00:20:01,760
Now will you shoot him?

257
00:20:01,830 --> 00:20:03,960
Open fire!

258
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,130
Hey!

259
00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:12,670
On his heels!

260
00:20:28,130 --> 00:20:30,060
Take cover, man!

261
00:20:30,130 --> 00:20:34,720
Gillette, Mr. Sparrow has a dawn appointment with the gallows.

262
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:37,360
I would hate for him to miss it.

263
00:20:40,440 --> 00:20:43,310
Search upstairs!

264
00:20:43,380 --> 00:20:45,210
Look lively, men!

265
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:28,210
Whoa!

266
00:22:17,800 --> 00:22:20,030
Right where I left you.

267
00:22:26,610 --> 00:22:29,100
Not where I left you.

268
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:40,650
You're the one they're hunting.

269
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:43,730
The pirate.

270
00:22:43,800 --> 00:22:46,790
You seem familiar. Have I threatened you before?

271
00:22:46,870 --> 00:22:50,630
I make a point of avoiding familiarity with pirates.

272
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:53,470
It would be a shame to put a mark on your record.

273
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:55,400
So if you'll excuse me.

274
00:22:59,110 --> 00:23:01,480
Do you think this wise, boy?

275
00:23:01,550 --> 00:23:04,670
Crossing blades with a pirate?

276
00:23:04,750 --> 00:23:07,010
You threatened Miss Swann.

277
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:12,020
Only a little.

278
00:23:24,170 --> 00:23:28,230
You know what you're doing. I'll give you that. Excellent form.

279
00:23:28,310 --> 00:23:30,770
But how's your footwork?

280
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:32,900
If I step here.

281
00:23:33,550 --> 00:23:34,940
Very good.

282
00:23:35,010 --> 00:23:37,040
And now I step again.

283
00:23:42,290 --> 00:23:43,150
Ta.

284
00:24:03,170 --> 00:24:05,770
That is a wonderful trick.

285
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:09,680
Except, once again, you are between me and my way out.

286
00:24:09,750 --> 00:24:12,880
And now you have no weapon.

287
00:24:35,270 --> 00:24:36,870
Who makes all these?

288
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:39,670
I do.

289
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:44,340
And I practice with them three hours a day.

290
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:47,610
You need to find yourself a girl, mate.

291
00:24:54,230 --> 00:24:57,220
Or perhaps the reason you practice three hours a day

292
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:58,630
is that you found one

293
00:24:58,730 --> 00:25:03,100
and are otherwise incapable of wooing said strumpet.

294
00:25:03,170 --> 00:25:05,360
You're not a eunuch, are you?

295
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:08,460
I practice three hours a day so when I meet a pirate,

296
00:25:08,610 --> 00:25:09,870
I can kill it!

297
00:26:27,190 --> 00:26:28,170
You cheated.

298
00:26:28,250 --> 00:26:29,520
Pirate.

299
00:26:33,090 --> 00:26:34,220
Move away.

300
00:26:34,290 --> 00:26:35,620
No.

301
00:26:35,690 --> 00:26:37,390
-Please move. -No.

302
00:26:37,460 --> 00:26:40,490
I cannot just step aside and let you escape.

303
00:26:42,970 --> 00:26:45,730
This shot is not meant for you.

304
00:26:55,210 --> 00:26:58,050
There he is! Over here!

305
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:02,380
Excellent work, Mr. Brown.

306
00:27:02,450 --> 00:27:05,180
You've assisted in the capture of a fugitive.

307
00:27:05,260 --> 00:27:08,560
Just doing my civic duty, sir.

308
00:27:08,630 --> 00:27:11,150
I trust you will remember this is the day

309
00:27:11,230 --> 00:27:14,860
Captain Jack Sparrow almost escaped.

310
00:27:14,930 --> 00:27:16,420
Take him away.

311
00:27:21,970 --> 00:27:23,440
-Can you smell it? -Come here.

312
00:27:23,500 --> 00:27:25,200
-It's marrowbone. -Come here.

313
00:27:25,270 --> 00:27:27,070
-Want a juicy bone? -Come here.

314
00:27:27,140 --> 00:27:29,970
-Come here, boy! -Come on.

315
00:27:30,040 --> 00:27:33,980
You can keep doing that forever. The dog is never going to move.

316
00:27:34,050 --> 00:27:35,170
Oh, excuse us

317
00:27:35,250 --> 00:27:38,510
if we haven't resigned ourself to the gallows just yet.

318
00:27:44,930 --> 00:27:46,620
There you go, miss.

319
00:27:46,690 --> 00:27:49,320
It was a difficult day for you, I'm sure.

320
00:27:49,400 --> 00:27:52,060
I suspected Commodore Norrington would propose.

321
00:27:52,130 --> 00:27:54,690
I must admit, I wasn't prepared for it.

322
00:27:54,770 --> 00:27:58,730
I meant you being threatened by that pirate. Sounds terrifying.

323
00:27:58,810 --> 00:28:00,300
Oh.

324
00:28:00,370 --> 00:28:02,140
Yes, it was terrifying.

325
00:28:02,210 --> 00:28:05,470
But the commodore proposed. Fancy that.

326
00:28:05,550 --> 00:28:09,280
Now, that's a smart match, miss, if it's not too bold to say.

327
00:28:09,350 --> 00:28:12,080
It is a smart match.

328
00:28:12,150 --> 00:28:14,140
He's a fine man.

329
00:28:15,160 --> 00:28:18,610
He's what any woman should dream of marrying.

330
00:28:18,690 --> 00:28:22,960
Well, that Will Turner. He's a fine man, too.

331
00:28:23,030 --> 00:28:24,960
That is too bold.

332
00:28:25,030 --> 00:28:28,930
Well, begging your pardon, miss. It was not my place.

333
00:29:17,750 --> 00:29:20,690
Has my daughter given you an answer yet?

334
00:29:20,750 --> 00:29:22,620
No, she hasn't.

335
00:29:22,690 --> 00:29:25,660
Well, she has had a very trying day.

336
00:29:26,730 --> 00:29:28,890
Ghastly weather, don't you think?

337
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:30,730
Bleak. Very bleak.

338
00:29:32,470 --> 00:29:34,560
What's that?

339
00:29:34,630 --> 00:29:36,630
Cannon fire!

340
00:29:37,700 --> 00:29:39,800
Return fire!

341
00:29:39,870 --> 00:29:42,500
-I know those guns. -Men to arms!

342
00:29:47,880 --> 00:29:49,870
It's the Pearl,

343
00:29:49,950 --> 00:29:52,010
The Black Pearl?

344
00:29:53,950 --> 00:29:55,780
I've heard stories.

345
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:59,620
She's been preying on ships and settlements for near 10 years.

346
00:29:59,690 --> 00:30:01,220
Never leaves any survivors.

347
00:30:01,290 --> 00:30:03,560
No survivors?

348
00:30:03,630 --> 00:30:06,600
Then where do the stories come from, I wonder?

349
00:30:45,340 --> 00:30:47,100
Coming through!

350
00:31:17,740 --> 00:31:19,640
Aaah!

351
00:31:38,590 --> 00:31:40,020
Sight the muzzle flash!

352
00:31:40,090 --> 00:31:41,250
Aim for the flashes!

353
00:31:41,330 --> 00:31:43,190
I need a full strike, fore and aft.

354
00:31:43,260 --> 00:31:45,820
Mr. Stephens, more cartridges!

355
00:31:45,900 --> 00:31:49,860
Governor, barricade yourself in my office.

356
00:31:51,170 --> 00:31:53,500
That's an order.

357
00:32:11,020 --> 00:32:12,360
Don't!

358
00:32:12,430 --> 00:32:14,190
Hello, chum.

359
00:32:18,130 --> 00:32:21,130
Up there.

360
00:32:29,640 --> 00:32:32,200
Miss Swann, they've come to kidnap you!

361
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:32,970
What?

362
00:32:33,050 --> 00:32:35,010
You're the governor's daughter.

363
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:36,550
In here!

364
00:32:37,880 --> 00:32:39,650
Listen. They haven't seen you.

365
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:42,150
The first chance you get, run to the fort.

366
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:52,190
Gotcha.

367
00:32:57,940 --> 00:33:00,240
No! No! No! It's hot!

368
00:33:00,310 --> 00:33:01,400
You burned me!

369
00:33:01,470 --> 00:33:02,700
Come on!

370
00:33:35,210 --> 00:33:36,000
No!

371
00:33:46,020 --> 00:33:49,960
We know you're here, poppet.

372
00:33:50,020 --> 00:33:51,120
Poppet.

373
00:33:51,190 --> 00:33:55,460
Come out and we promise we won't hurt you.

374
00:33:55,530 --> 00:33:56,690
Eh?

375
00:33:58,360 --> 00:34:01,490
We will find you, poppet.

376
00:34:01,570 --> 00:34:06,900
You've got something of ours, and it calls to us.

377
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:15,140
The gold calls to us.

378
00:34:15,210 --> 00:34:17,580
Gold.

379
00:34:22,990 --> 00:34:25,150
Hello, poppet.

380
00:34:25,220 --> 00:34:27,220
Parley.

381
00:34:27,290 --> 00:34:27,920
What?

382
00:34:27,990 --> 00:34:29,150
Parley.

383
00:34:29,230 --> 00:34:31,320
I invoke the right of parley.

384
00:34:31,400 --> 00:34:34,670
According to the Code of the Brethren set down by the pirates Morgan and Bartholomew,

385
00:34:34,730 --> 00:34:36,170
you have to take me to your captain.

386
00:34:36,240 --> 00:34:37,830
I know the Code.

387
00:34:37,900 --> 00:34:39,460
If an adversary demands parley,

388
00:34:39,540 --> 00:34:41,840
you can do them no harm until the parley is complete.

389
00:34:41,910 --> 00:34:43,210
To blazes with the Code.

390
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:46,250
She wants to be taken to the captain.

391
00:34:47,010 --> 00:34:50,280
And she'll go without a fuss.

392
00:34:50,350 --> 00:34:53,380
We must honor the Code.

393
00:35:04,060 --> 00:35:06,000
Say goodbye.

394
00:35:10,070 --> 00:35:11,370
Goodbye.

395
00:35:15,110 --> 00:35:16,510
-Will! -Come on!

396
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:17,980
Elizabeth.

397
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:28,780
Out of my way, scum!

398
00:35:40,830 --> 00:35:42,270
My sympathies, friend.

399
00:35:42,340 --> 00:35:44,570
You've no manner of luck at all.

400
00:36:06,330 --> 00:36:07,690
Come on, doggy.

401
00:36:07,760 --> 00:36:09,630
It's just you and me now.

402
00:36:09,700 --> 00:36:12,060
It's you and old Jack. Come on.

403
00:36:12,130 --> 00:36:13,860
Come on. That's a boy.

404
00:36:13,930 --> 00:36:16,090
Good boy. Come get the bone.

405
00:36:16,170 --> 00:36:17,760
That's a good boy. Come on.

406
00:36:17,840 --> 00:36:20,070
A bit closer. A bit closer.

407
00:36:20,140 --> 00:36:22,070
That's it. That's it, doggy.

408
00:36:22,140 --> 00:36:25,630
Come on, you filthy, slimy, mangy cur.

409
00:36:25,710 --> 00:36:27,440
-Don't do that.

410
00:36:27,510 --> 00:36:29,380
No, no! I didn't mean it!

411
00:36:29,450 --> 00:36:30,710
I didn't.

412
00:36:37,290 --> 00:36:39,280
This ain't the armory.

413
00:36:40,330 --> 00:36:44,590
Well, well, well. Look what we have here, Twigg.

414
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:47,160
Captain Jack Sparrow.

415
00:36:47,900 --> 00:36:50,060
Last time I saw you,

416
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:54,730
you were all alone on a godforsaken island,

417
00:36:54,810 --> 00:36:56,970
shrinking into the distance.

418
00:36:57,040 --> 00:36:59,570
His fortunes aren't improved much.

419
00:36:59,650 --> 00:37:03,010
Worry about your own fortunes, gentlemen.

420
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:08,190
The deepest circle of hell is reserved for betrayers and mutineers.

421
00:37:11,690 --> 00:37:13,990
So there is a curse.

422
00:37:15,460 --> 00:37:16,520
That's interesting.

423
00:37:16,600 --> 00:37:19,360
You know nothing of hell.

424
00:37:27,540 --> 00:37:29,910
That's very interesting.

425
00:38:04,710 --> 00:38:07,540
I didn't know we was taking on captives.

426
00:38:07,610 --> 00:38:10,780
She's invoked the right of parley with Captain Barbossa.

427
00:38:10,850 --> 00:38:14,580
-I'm here to -- -You'll speak when spoken to.

428
00:38:15,660 --> 00:38:17,120
And ye will not lay a hand

429
00:38:17,190 --> 00:38:20,680
on those under the protection of parley.

430
00:38:20,760 --> 00:38:22,490
Aye, sir.

431
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:27,300
My apologies, miss.

432
00:38:27,700 --> 00:38:30,900
Captain Barbossa, I am here to negotiate

433
00:38:30,970 --> 00:38:34,060
the cessation of hostilities against Port Royal.

434
00:38:34,140 --> 00:38:36,370
There were a lot of long words in there.

435
00:38:36,440 --> 00:38:39,540
We're naught but humble pirates.

436
00:38:39,610 --> 00:38:41,240
What is it that you want?

437
00:38:41,310 --> 00:38:43,540
I want you to leave and never come back.

438
00:38:46,050 --> 00:38:49,420
I'm disinclined to acquiesce to your request.

439
00:38:50,920 --> 00:38:52,580
Means "no."

440
00:38:53,960 --> 00:38:55,690
Very well.

441
00:38:57,730 --> 00:38:59,630
I'll drop it.

442
00:39:03,670 --> 00:39:05,830
Me holds are bursting with swag.

443
00:39:05,910 --> 00:39:08,600
That bit of shine matters to us?

444
00:39:08,670 --> 00:39:10,370
Why?

445
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:14,310
It's what you've been searching for. I recognize this ship.

446
00:39:14,380 --> 00:39:17,440
I saw it eight years ago on the crossing from England.

447
00:39:17,520 --> 00:39:19,880
Did you, now?

448
00:39:21,620 --> 00:39:23,420
Fine.

449
00:39:23,490 --> 00:39:25,220
I suppose if it is worthless,

450
00:39:25,290 --> 00:39:27,380
there's no point in me keeping it.

451
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:41,570
You have a name, missy?

452
00:39:42,540 --> 00:39:45,530
Elizabeth Turner.

453
00:39:46,550 --> 00:39:48,950
I'm a maid in the governor's household.

454
00:39:51,550 --> 00:39:53,750
Miss Turner.

455
00:39:55,550 --> 00:39:57,280
Bootstrap.

456
00:40:00,890 --> 00:40:03,520
How does a maid own a trinket such as that?

457
00:40:03,600 --> 00:40:07,030
Family heirloom, perhaps?

458
00:40:07,100 --> 00:40:10,030
I didn't steal it, if that's what you mean.

459
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:11,470
Very well. Hand it over.

460
00:40:11,540 --> 00:40:15,560
We'll put your town to our rudder and ne'er return.

461
00:40:28,690 --> 00:40:30,350
Our bargain?

462
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:35,080
Still the guns and stow them.

463
00:40:36,300 --> 00:40:39,290
Wait! You have to take me to shore!

464
00:40:39,370 --> 00:40:40,730
According to the Code --

465
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:45,400
First, your return to shore was not part of our agreement.

466
00:40:45,470 --> 00:40:46,730
So I must do nothing.

467
00:40:46,810 --> 00:40:49,740
You must be a pirate for the Pirate's Code to apply,

468
00:40:49,810 --> 00:40:50,740
and you're not.

469
00:40:50,810 --> 00:40:54,470
Thirdly, the Code is more what you'd call guidelines

470
00:40:54,550 --> 00:40:56,210
than actual rules.

471
00:40:56,280 --> 00:41:00,050
Welcome aboard the Black Pearl, Miss Turner.

472
00:41:20,670 --> 00:41:23,070
They've taken her. They've taken Elizabeth.

473
00:41:23,140 --> 00:41:26,270
Mr. Murtogg, remove this man.

474
00:41:26,350 --> 00:41:28,870
We have to hunt them down. We must save her.

475
00:41:28,950 --> 00:41:31,470
And where do you propose we start?

476
00:41:31,550 --> 00:41:34,180
If you have information concerning my daughter,

477
00:41:34,250 --> 00:41:36,190
please share it.

478
00:41:37,690 --> 00:41:41,850
That Jack Sparrow. He talked about the Black Pearl,

479
00:41:41,930 --> 00:41:44,520
Mentioned it is more what he did.

480
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:48,460
Ask him where it is. He could lead us to it.

481
00:41:48,530 --> 00:41:51,990
No. The pirates who invaded this fort

482
00:41:52,070 --> 00:41:56,530
left Sparrow locked in his cell, ergo they are not his allies.

483
00:41:56,610 --> 00:41:59,510
We will establish their most likely course.

484
00:41:59,580 --> 00:42:01,480
That's not good enough!

485
00:42:01,550 --> 00:42:05,380
Mr. Turner, you are not a military man.

486
00:42:05,450 --> 00:42:07,110
You are not a sailor.

487
00:42:08,150 --> 00:42:09,490
You are a blacksmith.

488
00:42:09,560 --> 00:42:13,220
And this is not the moment for rash actions.

489
00:42:14,790 --> 00:42:16,390
Do not make the mistake of thinking

490
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:20,020
you are the only man here who cares for Elizabeth.

491
00:42:31,410 --> 00:42:33,740
You. Sparrow.

492
00:42:33,810 --> 00:42:36,840
You are familiar with that ship. The Black Pearl,

493
00:42:36,920 --> 00:42:37,850
I've heard of it.

494
00:42:37,920 --> 00:42:41,440
Where does it make berth?

495
00:42:41,520 --> 00:42:44,010
Have you not heard the stories?

496
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:49,390
Captain Barbossa and his crew of miscreants

497
00:42:49,460 --> 00:42:52,090
sail from the dreaded lsla de Muerta.

498
00:42:52,160 --> 00:42:53,760
It's an island that cannot be found,

499
00:42:53,830 --> 00:42:57,700
except by those who already know where it is.

500
00:42:57,770 --> 00:43:00,200
The ship's real enough.

501
00:43:00,270 --> 00:43:02,210
lts anchorage must be a real place.

502
00:43:02,270 --> 00:43:03,670
Where is it?

503
00:43:03,740 --> 00:43:05,800
Why ask me?

504
00:43:05,880 --> 00:43:07,350
Because you're a pirate.

505
00:43:07,410 --> 00:43:10,250
And you want to turn pirate yourself, is that it?

506
00:43:11,350 --> 00:43:12,340
Never.

507
00:43:16,020 --> 00:43:17,250
They took Miss Swann.

508
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:19,690
Oh, so it is that you found a girl.

509
00:43:19,760 --> 00:43:22,890
I see. Well, if you're intending to brave all,

510
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:27,190
hasten to her rescue, and so win fair lady's heart,

511
00:43:27,270 --> 00:43:29,530
you'll have to do it alone, mate.

512
00:43:29,600 --> 00:43:32,040
I see no profit in it for me.

513
00:43:32,100 --> 00:43:33,570
I can get you out of here.

514
00:43:33,640 --> 00:43:35,830
How's that? They key's run off.

515
00:43:35,910 --> 00:43:37,500
I helped build these cells.

516
00:43:37,580 --> 00:43:39,810
These are half-pin barrel hinges.

517
00:43:41,210 --> 00:43:45,120
With the right leverage and the proper application of strength.

518
00:43:45,550 --> 00:43:47,640
The door will lift free.

519
00:43:49,320 --> 00:43:51,350
What's your name?

520
00:43:51,420 --> 00:43:53,650
Will Turner.

521
00:43:53,730 --> 00:43:56,130
That would be short for William, I imagine.

522
00:43:56,200 --> 00:43:58,290
Good, strong name.

523
00:43:58,360 --> 00:44:01,130
No doubt named for your father.

524
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:03,090
Yes.

525
00:44:03,170 --> 00:44:05,760
Uh-huh.

526
00:44:05,840 --> 00:44:09,140
Well, Mr. Turner, I've changed me mind.

527
00:44:09,210 --> 00:44:12,470
If you spring me from this cell, I swear on pain of death

528
00:44:12,550 --> 00:44:14,510
I shall take you to the Black Pearl

529
00:44:14,580 --> 00:44:16,670
and your bonny lass.

530
00:44:16,750 --> 00:44:19,120
Do we have an accord?

531
00:44:21,920 --> 00:44:22,580
Agreed.

532
00:44:22,660 --> 00:44:23,590
Agreed.

533
00:44:23,660 --> 00:44:25,280
Get me out.

534
00:44:28,360 --> 00:44:29,880
Hurry. Someone will have heard that.

535
00:44:29,960 --> 00:44:32,190
Not without my effects.

536
00:44:38,700 --> 00:44:40,470
We're going to steal the ship?

537
00:44:42,310 --> 00:44:43,070
That ship?

538
00:44:43,140 --> 00:44:44,940
Commandeer.

539
00:44:45,010 --> 00:44:47,270
We're going to commandeer that ship.

540
00:44:47,350 --> 00:44:49,280
Nautical term.

541
00:44:49,350 --> 00:44:51,250
One question about your business.

542
00:44:51,320 --> 00:44:52,680
Or there's no use going.

543
00:44:52,750 --> 00:44:54,380
This girl.

544
00:44:54,450 --> 00:44:57,120
How far are you willing to go to save her?

545
00:44:57,190 --> 00:44:58,620
I'd die for her.

546
00:44:58,690 --> 00:44:59,950
Oh, good.

547
00:45:00,030 --> 00:45:01,820
No worries, then.

548
00:45:20,880 --> 00:45:24,250
This is either madness or brilliance.

549
00:45:24,320 --> 00:45:28,980
It's remarkable how often those two traits coincide.

550
00:45:57,420 --> 00:46:01,320
Everyone stay calm! We are taking over the ship.

551
00:46:01,390 --> 00:46:03,380
Aye! Avast!

552
00:46:06,290 --> 00:46:09,390
This ship cannot be crewed by two men.

553
00:46:09,460 --> 00:46:12,120
You'll never make it out of the bay.

554
00:46:12,200 --> 00:46:16,860
Son, I'm Captain Jack Sparrow.

555
00:46:16,940 --> 00:46:18,490
Savvy?

556
00:46:22,040 --> 00:46:23,070
Commodore.

557
00:46:23,140 --> 00:46:26,040
LT. GlLLETTE: Sir, they've taken the Dauntless!

558
00:46:27,510 --> 00:46:29,240
Commodore!

559
00:46:29,310 --> 00:46:31,810
They've taken the ship!

560
00:46:31,880 --> 00:46:34,110
Sparrow and Turner have taken the Dauntless!

561
00:46:34,190 --> 00:46:38,050
Rash, Turner. Too rash.

562
00:46:38,860 --> 00:46:43,690
That is without doubt the worst pirate I have ever seen.

563
00:46:46,170 --> 00:46:47,690
Here they come.

564
00:46:52,340 --> 00:46:55,700
Bring her around! Bring her around!

565
00:47:01,310 --> 00:47:05,180
Search every cabin, every hull, down to the bilges.

566
00:47:20,400 --> 00:47:22,230
Sailors, back to the Interceptor,

567
00:47:22,300 --> 00:47:23,360
Now!

568
00:47:29,440 --> 00:47:33,310
Thank you, Commodore, for getting us ready to make way.

569
00:47:33,380 --> 00:47:36,680
We'd have had a hard time of it by ourselves.

570
00:47:39,180 --> 00:47:41,550
Set topsails and clear up this mess.

571
00:47:41,620 --> 00:47:43,550
With the wind, we won't catch them.

572
00:47:43,620 --> 00:47:46,420
I don't need to. Get them in range of the long nines.

573
00:47:46,490 --> 00:47:48,260
Hands, come about!

574
00:47:48,330 --> 00:47:50,490
Run out the guns!

575
00:47:50,560 --> 00:47:53,160
We are to fire on our own ship, sir?

576
00:47:53,230 --> 00:47:55,220
I'd rather see her at the bottom of the ocean

577
00:47:55,300 --> 00:47:57,290
than in the hands of a pirate.

578
00:47:58,340 --> 00:48:02,400
Commodore, he's disabled the rudder chain, sir.

579
00:48:05,410 --> 00:48:07,440
Abandon ship!

580
00:48:13,750 --> 00:48:17,350
That's got to be the best pirate I've ever seen.

581
00:48:17,420 --> 00:48:20,020
So it would seem.

582
00:48:28,570 --> 00:48:30,590
WlLL: When I was a lad living in England,

583
00:48:30,670 --> 00:48:32,600
my mother raised me by herself.

584
00:48:32,670 --> 00:48:37,300
After she died, I came out here, looking for my father.

585
00:48:37,910 --> 00:48:39,880
Is that so?

586
00:48:40,880 --> 00:48:43,810
My father, Will Turner.

587
00:48:43,880 --> 00:48:46,870
It was only after you learnt my name you agreed to help.

588
00:48:46,950 --> 00:48:49,320
Since that's what I wanted, I didn't press the matter.

589
00:48:49,390 --> 00:48:51,380
I'm not a simpleton, Jack.

590
00:48:51,460 --> 00:48:53,480
You knew my father.

591
00:48:59,500 --> 00:49:00,760
I knew him.

592
00:49:00,830 --> 00:49:03,990
Probably one of the few who knew him as William Turner.

593
00:49:04,070 --> 00:49:07,160
Everyone else called him Bootstrap or Bootstrap Bill.

594
00:49:07,240 --> 00:49:09,070
Bootstrap?

595
00:49:09,140 --> 00:49:11,700
Good man. Good pirate.

596
00:49:11,780 --> 00:49:13,870
I swear, you look just like him.

597
00:49:13,940 --> 00:49:14,970
It's not true.

598
00:49:15,050 --> 00:49:16,950
He was a merchant sailor.

599
00:49:17,010 --> 00:49:19,180
A respectable man who obeyed the law.

600
00:49:19,250 --> 00:49:22,690
He was a bloody pirate. A scallywag.

601
00:49:22,750 --> 00:49:26,620
My father was not a pirate.

602
00:49:26,690 --> 00:49:30,680
Put it away, son. It's not worth you getting beat again.

603
00:49:30,760 --> 00:49:33,700
You ignored the rules of engagement.

604
00:49:33,760 --> 00:49:35,250
In a fair fight, I'd kill you.

605
00:49:35,330 --> 00:49:38,360
Then that's not much incentive for me to fight fair.

606
00:49:45,480 --> 00:49:48,810
Now, as long as you're just hanging there, pay attention.

607
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:51,750
The only rules that really matter are these.

608
00:49:51,820 --> 00:49:55,270
What a man can do and what a man can't do.

609
00:49:55,350 --> 00:49:56,720
For instance,

610
00:49:56,790 --> 00:50:00,280
you can accept that your father was a pirate and a good man

611
00:50:00,360 --> 00:50:01,520
or you can't.

612
00:50:01,590 --> 00:50:03,030
Pirate is in your blood,

613
00:50:03,090 --> 00:50:05,690
so you'll have to square with that someday.

614
00:50:05,760 --> 00:50:06,920
Now, me, for example.

615
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:08,900
I can let you drown.

616
00:50:08,970 --> 00:50:11,200
But I can't bring this ship into Tortuga

617
00:50:11,270 --> 00:50:12,700
all by me onesy, savvy?

618
00:50:12,770 --> 00:50:14,070
So.

619
00:50:18,940 --> 00:50:22,400
Can you sail under the command of a pirate?

620
00:50:23,550 --> 00:50:25,450
Or can you not?

621
00:50:27,580 --> 00:50:29,480
Tortuga?

622
00:50:29,550 --> 00:50:30,950
Tortuga.

623
00:50:53,080 --> 00:50:55,600
More importantly, it is indeed a sad life

624
00:50:55,680 --> 00:50:58,650
that has never breathed deep this sweet bouquet

625
00:50:58,720 --> 00:51:00,880
that is Tortuga, savvy?

626
00:51:01,950 --> 00:51:04,080
What do you think?

627
00:51:07,460 --> 00:51:08,520
lt'll linger.

628
00:51:08,590 --> 00:51:10,290
I'll tell you, mate,

629
00:51:10,360 --> 00:51:13,160
if every town in the world were like this one,

630
00:51:13,230 --> 00:51:16,600
no man would ever feel unwanted.

631
00:51:16,670 --> 00:51:18,160
Scarlett!

632
00:51:20,240 --> 00:51:22,170
Not sure I deserved that.

633
00:51:23,210 --> 00:51:24,530
Giselle!

634
00:51:24,610 --> 00:51:25,540
Who was she?

635
00:51:25,610 --> 00:51:26,470
What?

636
00:51:28,980 --> 00:51:30,880
I may have deserved that.

637
00:51:32,380 --> 00:51:35,410
Curse you for breathing, you slack-jawed idiot!

638
00:51:37,950 --> 00:51:39,850
Mother's love!

639
00:51:39,920 --> 00:51:41,820
Jack!

640
00:51:41,890 --> 00:51:45,120
You should know better than to wake a man when he's sleeping.

641
00:51:45,200 --> 00:51:46,530
It's bad luck.

642
00:51:46,600 --> 00:51:48,890
Fortunately, I know how to counter it.

643
00:51:48,970 --> 00:51:50,230
The man who did the waking

644
00:51:50,300 --> 00:51:52,560
buys the man who was sleeping a drink.

645
00:51:52,640 --> 00:51:55,430
The man who was sleeping drinks while listening to a proposition

646
00:51:55,510 --> 00:51:57,800
from the man who did the waking.

647
00:51:59,880 --> 00:52:02,540
Aye, that'll about do it.

648
00:52:06,680 --> 00:52:08,910
Blast! I'm already awake!

649
00:52:08,990 --> 00:52:10,950
That was for the smell.

650
00:52:20,800 --> 00:52:22,730
Keep a sharp eye.

651
00:52:29,470 --> 00:52:33,700
Now, what's the nature of this venture of yourn?

652
00:52:34,850 --> 00:52:37,040
I'm going after the Black Pearl,

653
00:52:40,880 --> 00:52:45,320
I know where it's going to be, and I'm gonna take it.

654
00:52:45,390 --> 00:52:48,550
Jack, it's a fool's errand.

655
00:52:48,630 --> 00:52:51,960
You know better than me the tales of the Black Pearl,

656
00:52:52,030 --> 00:52:55,120
That's why I know what Barbossa is up to.

657
00:52:55,200 --> 00:52:57,600
All I need is a crew.

658
00:52:57,670 --> 00:52:59,190
From what I hear of Barbossa,

659
00:52:59,270 --> 00:53:02,100
he's not a man to suffer fools, nor strike a bargain with one.

660
00:53:02,170 --> 00:53:04,640
I'd say it's a good thing I'm not a fool.

661
00:53:04,710 --> 00:53:06,300
Prove me wrong.

662
00:53:06,380 --> 00:53:09,870
What makes you think Barbossa will give up his ship to you?

663
00:53:09,950 --> 00:53:12,780
Let's just say it's a matter of leverage.

664
00:53:29,500 --> 00:53:31,130
The kid?

665
00:53:32,070 --> 00:53:36,100
That is the child of Bootstrap Bill Turner.

666
00:53:36,170 --> 00:53:38,640
His only child, savvy?

667
00:53:38,710 --> 00:53:40,610
Is he, now?

668
00:53:43,550 --> 00:53:45,950
"Leverage," says you.

669
00:53:46,020 --> 00:53:48,950
"I think I feel a change in the wind," says i.

670
00:53:49,020 --> 00:53:50,580
I'll find us a crew.

671
00:53:50,650 --> 00:53:53,420
There's bound to be some sailors crazy as you.

672
00:53:53,490 --> 00:53:55,690
One can only hope.

673
00:53:55,760 --> 00:53:56,850
Take what you can.

674
00:53:56,930 --> 00:53:59,360
Give nothing back.

675
00:54:23,390 --> 00:54:25,290
You'll be dining with the captain.

676
00:54:25,360 --> 00:54:28,480
And he requests you wear this.

677
00:54:28,560 --> 00:54:29,750
You tell the captain

678
00:54:29,830 --> 00:54:33,230
that I am disinclined to acquiesce to his request.

679
00:54:33,300 --> 00:54:35,660
He said you'd say that.

680
00:54:35,730 --> 00:54:38,200
He also said, if that be the case,

681
00:54:38,270 --> 00:54:40,100
you'll be dining with the crew.

682
00:54:40,170 --> 00:54:42,640
And you'll be naked.

683
00:54:48,310 --> 00:54:49,400
Fine.

684
00:55:05,930 --> 00:55:10,660
There's no need to stand on ceremony, nor call to impress.

685
00:55:10,730 --> 00:55:12,790
You must be hungry.

686
00:55:29,490 --> 00:55:31,390
Try the wine.

687
00:55:35,690 --> 00:55:36,750
And the apples.

688
00:55:36,830 --> 00:55:39,060
One of those next?

689
00:55:46,840 --> 00:55:48,530
It's poisoned.

690
00:55:50,170 --> 00:55:52,370
There would be no sense to be killing you.

691
00:55:52,440 --> 00:55:54,030
Release me. You have your trinket.

692
00:55:54,110 --> 00:55:56,640
I'm of no further value to you.

693
00:55:59,920 --> 00:56:03,480
You don't know what this is, do you?

694
00:56:03,550 --> 00:56:05,210
It's a pirate medallion.

695
00:56:05,290 --> 00:56:08,720
This is Aztec gold.

696
00:56:08,790 --> 00:56:11,320
One of 882 identical pieces

697
00:56:11,390 --> 00:56:16,090
they delivered in a stone chest to Cortes himself.

698
00:56:16,170 --> 00:56:18,290
Blood money paid to stem the slaughter

699
00:56:18,370 --> 00:56:21,000
he wreaked upon them with his armies.

700
00:56:21,070 --> 00:56:24,400
But the greed of Cortes was insatiable.

701
00:56:24,470 --> 00:56:32,140
So the heathen gods placed upon the gold a terrible curse.

702
00:56:33,050 --> 00:56:34,640
Any mortal that removes

703
00:56:34,720 --> 00:56:38,120
but a single piece from that stone chest

704
00:56:38,190 --> 00:56:41,590
shall be punished for eternity.

705
00:56:43,090 --> 00:56:46,490
I hardly believe in ghost stories anymore, Captain.

706
00:56:46,560 --> 00:56:48,460
Aye.

707
00:56:48,530 --> 00:56:52,130
That's exactly what I thought when we were told the tale.

708
00:56:52,200 --> 00:56:54,970
Buried on an island of dead what cannot be found,

709
00:56:55,040 --> 00:56:57,730
except for those who know where it is.

710
00:56:58,640 --> 00:57:00,440
Find it, we did.

711
00:57:01,650 --> 00:57:03,410
There be the chest.

712
00:57:03,480 --> 00:57:05,850
lnside be the gold.

713
00:57:05,920 --> 00:57:08,320
And we took them all.

714
00:57:08,390 --> 00:57:10,480
We spent them and traded them

715
00:57:10,550 --> 00:57:13,040
and frittered them away on drink and food

716
00:57:13,120 --> 00:57:14,890
and pleasurable company.

717
00:57:14,960 --> 00:57:21,160
The more we gave them away, the more we came to realize,

718
00:57:21,230 --> 00:57:24,330
the drink would not satisfy,

719
00:57:24,400 --> 00:57:27,930
food turned to ash in our mouths,

720
00:57:28,000 --> 00:57:30,330
and all the pleasurable company in the world

721
00:57:30,410 --> 00:57:32,900
could not slake our lust.

722
00:57:34,510 --> 00:57:37,910
We are cursed men, Miss Turner.

723
00:57:38,880 --> 00:57:45,010
Compelled by greed, we were, but now we are consumed by it.

724
00:57:56,170 --> 00:57:59,830
There is one way we can end our curse.

725
00:58:03,040 --> 00:58:06,200
All the scattered pieces of the Aztec gold

726
00:58:06,280 --> 00:58:10,840
must be restored and the blood repaid.

727
00:58:12,450 --> 00:58:17,250
Thanks to ye, we have the final piece.

728
00:58:18,520 --> 00:58:20,790
And the blood to be repaid?

729
00:58:20,860 --> 00:58:25,920
That's why there's no sense to be killin' you yet.

730
00:58:27,830 --> 00:58:29,460
Apple?

731
00:58:35,640 --> 00:58:36,700
Arr!

732
00:58:37,840 --> 00:58:39,470
No!

733
00:58:45,650 --> 00:58:46,710
I'm curious.

734
00:58:46,780 --> 00:58:50,310
After killing me, what is it you're planning on doing next?

735
00:58:56,190 --> 00:58:58,720
Aah!

736
00:59:48,240 --> 00:59:49,040
Aaaah!

737
00:59:54,120 --> 00:59:55,380
Look!

738
00:59:55,450 --> 00:59:59,010
The moonlight shows us for what we really are.

739
00:59:59,090 --> 01:00:02,280
We are not among the living, and so we cannot die.

740
01:00:02,360 --> 01:00:04,550
But neither are we dead.

741
01:00:05,230 --> 01:00:09,190
For too long I've been parched and unable to quench it.

742
01:00:09,270 --> 01:00:14,130
Too long I've been starving to death and haven't died.

743
01:00:14,200 --> 01:00:16,540
I feel nothing.

744
01:00:16,610 --> 01:00:20,370
Not the wind on my face nor the spray of the sea

745
01:00:20,440 --> 01:00:24,040
nor the warmth of a woman's flesh.

746
01:00:24,810 --> 01:00:28,580
You best start believing in ghost stories, Miss Turner.

747
01:00:28,650 --> 01:00:30,380
You're in one!

748
01:00:48,770 --> 01:00:53,680
What are you looking at? Back to work!

749
01:00:59,950 --> 01:01:01,850
Feast your eyes, Captain.

750
01:01:01,920 --> 01:01:04,480
All of them faithful hands before the mast.

751
01:01:04,550 --> 01:01:07,890
Every man worth his salt.

752
01:01:07,960 --> 01:01:09,950
And crazy to boot.

753
01:01:10,030 --> 01:01:13,480
So this is your able-bodied crew?

754
01:01:19,070 --> 01:01:21,200
-You, sailor! -Cotton, sir.

755
01:01:21,270 --> 01:01:24,970
Mr. Cotton, do you have the courage to follow orders

756
01:01:25,040 --> 01:01:28,030
and stay true in the face of danger and death?

757
01:01:28,110 --> 01:01:31,380
Mr. Cotton! Answer, man!

758
01:01:31,450 --> 01:01:34,080
He's a mute. Poor devil had his tongue cut out.

759
01:01:34,150 --> 01:01:36,410
He trained the parrot to talk for him.

760
01:01:36,490 --> 01:01:38,920
No one's yet figured how.

761
01:01:41,560 --> 01:01:44,390
Mr. Cotton's parrot.

762
01:01:44,460 --> 01:01:45,720
Same question.

763
01:01:45,800 --> 01:01:49,200
Wind in the sails! Wind in the sails!

764
01:01:49,670 --> 01:01:52,000
Mostly we figure that means "yes."

765
01:01:52,070 --> 01:01:53,260
Of course it does.

766
01:01:53,340 --> 01:01:55,200
Satisfied?

767
01:01:55,270 --> 01:01:57,430
Well, you've proved they're mad.

768
01:01:57,510 --> 01:02:00,000
And what's the benefit for us?

769
01:02:09,750 --> 01:02:11,580
Anamaria.

770
01:02:13,360 --> 01:02:15,150
I suppose you didn't deserve that.

771
01:02:15,220 --> 01:02:17,160
No, that one I deserved.

772
01:02:17,230 --> 01:02:19,420
You stole my boat!

773
01:02:19,500 --> 01:02:22,160
Actually --

774
01:02:22,230 --> 01:02:25,030
Borrowed. Borrowed without permission.

775
01:02:25,100 --> 01:02:27,130
But with every intention of bringing it back.

776
01:02:27,200 --> 01:02:28,690
But you didn't!

777
01:02:28,770 --> 01:02:31,640
You'll get another one.

778
01:02:31,710 --> 01:02:32,540
I will.

779
01:02:32,610 --> 01:02:34,100
A better one.

780
01:02:34,180 --> 01:02:35,840
A better one!

781
01:02:35,910 --> 01:02:36,710
That one.

782
01:02:36,780 --> 01:02:38,410
What one?

783
01:02:40,380 --> 01:02:42,150
That one?

784
01:02:44,950 --> 01:02:46,720
Aye, that one.

785
01:02:46,790 --> 01:02:48,380
-What say you? -Aye!

786
01:02:48,460 --> 01:02:49,860
Aye!

787
01:02:49,930 --> 01:02:51,720
Anchors aweigh!

788
01:02:51,790 --> 01:02:56,090
No, no, it's frightful bad luck to bring a woman aboard, sir.

789
01:02:56,170 --> 01:02:58,400
lt'll be far worse not to have her.

790
01:03:32,070 --> 01:03:35,660
How can we sail to an island that nobody can find

791
01:03:35,740 --> 01:03:37,670
with a compass that doesn't work?

792
01:03:37,740 --> 01:03:39,830
Aye, the compass doesn't point North,

793
01:03:39,910 --> 01:03:43,470
but we're not trying to find North, are we?

794
01:03:53,360 --> 01:03:55,790
We should drop canvas, sir.

795
01:03:55,860 --> 01:03:58,690
She can hold a bit longer.

796
01:04:00,100 --> 01:04:03,730
What's in your head that's put you in such a fine mood?

797
01:04:03,800 --> 01:04:05,770
We're catching up.

798
01:04:20,450 --> 01:04:22,780
Time to go, poppet.

799
01:04:22,850 --> 01:04:26,220
Ho! Ho! Ho! Ho! Ho! Ho!

800
01:05:18,140 --> 01:05:23,440
Dead men tell no tales.

801
01:05:25,180 --> 01:05:26,580
Puts a chill in the bones

802
01:05:26,650 --> 01:05:30,580
how many honest sailors have been claimed by this passage.

803
01:05:45,200 --> 01:05:47,600
How is it that Jack came by that compass?

804
01:05:47,670 --> 01:05:50,760
Not a lot's known about Jack fore he showed up in Tortuga

805
01:05:50,840 --> 01:05:53,700
with a mind to go after the treasure of the lsla de Muerta.

806
01:05:53,770 --> 01:05:55,030
That was before I'd met him.

807
01:05:55,110 --> 01:05:58,100
Back when he was Captain of the Black Pearl,

808
01:05:58,180 --> 01:05:59,170
What?

809
01:06:02,650 --> 01:06:04,310
He failed to mention that.

810
01:06:04,380 --> 01:06:06,610
He plays things closer to the vest now.

811
01:06:06,690 --> 01:06:08,740
And a hard-learned lesson it was.

812
01:06:08,820 --> 01:06:11,290
Three days out, the first mate comes to him

813
01:06:11,360 --> 01:06:13,420
and says everything's an equal share.

814
01:06:13,490 --> 01:06:15,960
That should mean the location of the treasure.

815
01:06:16,030 --> 01:06:18,330
Jack gives up the bearings.

816
01:06:18,400 --> 01:06:21,560
That night, there was a mutiny.

817
01:06:21,630 --> 01:06:24,760
They marooned Jack on an island and left him to die,

818
01:06:24,840 --> 01:06:27,930
but not before he'd gone mad with the heat.

819
01:06:31,280 --> 01:06:33,710
So that's the reason for all the...

820
01:06:35,810 --> 01:06:38,250
Reason's got nothing to do with it.

821
01:06:38,320 --> 01:06:40,580
Now, Will, when a pirate's marooned,

822
01:06:40,650 --> 01:06:43,710
he's a given a pistol with a single shot. One shot.

823
01:06:43,790 --> 01:06:46,920
Well, that won't do much good hunting or to be rescued.

824
01:06:46,990 --> 01:06:50,430
But after three weeks of a starving belly and thirst,

825
01:06:50,500 --> 01:06:53,590
that pistol will start to look real friendly.

826
01:06:53,670 --> 01:06:57,260
But Jack escaped the island, and he still has that single shot.

827
01:06:57,340 --> 01:07:00,770
Oh, he won't use it, though, save on one man.

828
01:07:00,840 --> 01:07:02,970
His mutinous first mate.

829
01:07:03,040 --> 01:07:05,370
-Barbossa. -Aye.

830
01:07:06,210 --> 01:07:07,940
How did Jack get off the island?

831
01:07:08,010 --> 01:07:09,540
Well, I'll tell ye.

832
01:07:09,610 --> 01:07:11,410
He waded out into the shallows.

833
01:07:11,480 --> 01:07:13,950
He waited there three days and three nights

834
01:07:14,020 --> 01:07:15,850
till all manner of sea creatures

835
01:07:15,920 --> 01:07:18,690
'came acclimated to his presence.

836
01:07:18,760 --> 01:07:20,490
And on the fourth morning,

837
01:07:20,560 --> 01:07:22,690
he roped a couple of sea turtles,

838
01:07:22,760 --> 01:07:25,990
lashed them together, and made a raft.

839
01:07:27,000 --> 01:07:28,760
He roped a couple of sea turtles?

840
01:07:28,830 --> 01:07:31,500
Aye, sea turtles.

841
01:07:33,040 --> 01:07:34,970
What did he use for rope?

842
01:07:40,250 --> 01:07:42,440
Human hair.

843
01:07:42,510 --> 01:07:44,310
From my back.

844
01:07:45,320 --> 01:07:46,480
Let go the anchor!

845
01:07:46,550 --> 01:07:48,990
Aye, Captain, aye!

846
01:07:49,050 --> 01:07:52,020
Young Mr. Turner and l are to go ashore.

847
01:07:52,090 --> 01:07:55,720
Captain, what if the worst should happen?

848
01:07:55,790 --> 01:07:56,920
Keep to the Code.

849
01:07:57,000 --> 01:07:59,090
Aye, the Code.

850
01:08:24,220 --> 01:08:25,420
Move.

851
01:08:25,490 --> 01:08:27,790
10 years of hoarding swag.

852
01:08:27,860 --> 01:08:30,350
And now we finally get to spend it.

853
01:08:36,870 --> 01:08:40,770
Once we're quit of the curse, we'll be rich men.

854
01:08:40,840 --> 01:08:44,540
You can buy an eye what actually fits and is made of glass.

855
01:08:44,610 --> 01:08:47,580
This one does splinter something terrible.

856
01:08:48,410 --> 01:08:50,570
Stop rubbing it.

857
01:09:09,090 --> 01:09:12,080
What Code is Gibbs to keep to if the worst should happen?

858
01:09:12,160 --> 01:09:14,290
Pirate's Code.

859
01:09:14,360 --> 01:09:18,990
Any man who falls behind is left behind.

860
01:09:19,670 --> 01:09:22,600
No heroes amongst thieves?

861
01:09:23,270 --> 01:09:25,740
For having such a bleak outlook on pirates,

862
01:09:25,810 --> 01:09:28,680
you're well on your way to becoming one.

863
01:09:28,740 --> 01:09:31,680
Sprung a man from jail,

864
01:09:31,750 --> 01:09:34,110
commandeered a ship of the fleet,

865
01:09:34,180 --> 01:09:37,520
sailed with a buccaneer crew out of Tortuga.

866
01:09:39,220 --> 01:09:42,590
And you're completely obsessed with treasure.

867
01:09:45,330 --> 01:09:49,700
That's not true. I am not obsessed with treasure.

868
01:09:56,170 --> 01:09:58,940
Not all treasure is silver and gold, mate.

869
01:09:59,010 --> 01:10:01,810
Gentlemen, the time has come!.

870
01:10:01,880 --> 01:10:04,870
Our salvation is nigh!

871
01:10:05,750 --> 01:10:07,810
Our torment is near an end.

872
01:10:07,880 --> 01:10:09,480
Elizabeth.

873
01:10:09,550 --> 01:10:12,150
For 10 years we've been tested and tried,

874
01:10:12,220 --> 01:10:14,020
and each man jack of you here

875
01:10:14,090 --> 01:10:17,460
has proved his mettle a hundred times over

876
01:10:17,530 --> 01:10:20,190
and a hundred times again!

877
01:10:21,700 --> 01:10:24,000
Suffered, I have!

878
01:10:24,070 --> 01:10:27,400
Punished, we were, the lot of us.

879
01:10:27,470 --> 01:10:31,570
Disproportionate to our crime!

880
01:10:32,780 --> 01:10:34,740
Here it is!

881
01:10:37,680 --> 01:10:41,310
The cursed treasure of Cortes himself.

882
01:10:42,990 --> 01:10:47,650
Every last piece that went astray, we have returned.

883
01:10:47,720 --> 01:10:49,380
Save for this.

884
01:10:49,460 --> 01:10:50,890
Jack!

885
01:10:50,960 --> 01:10:54,760
Not yet. We wait for the opportune moment.

886
01:10:54,830 --> 01:10:58,990
881 we found, but despaired of ever finding the last.

887
01:10:59,070 --> 01:11:00,870
When's that?

888
01:11:00,940 --> 01:11:03,460
When it's of greatest profit to you?

889
01:11:03,540 --> 01:11:06,270
May I ask you something?

890
01:11:06,340 --> 01:11:09,970
Have I ever given you reason not to trust me?

891
01:11:10,050 --> 01:11:11,380
Do us a favor.

892
01:11:11,450 --> 01:11:15,410
I know it's difficult for you, but please stay here

893
01:11:15,480 --> 01:11:18,480
and try not to do anything stupid.

894
01:11:21,020 --> 01:11:25,150
And who among us has paid the blood sacrifice owed to the heathen gods?

895
01:11:25,230 --> 01:11:25,890
Us!

896
01:11:25,960 --> 01:11:28,790
And whose blood must yet be paid?

897
01:11:28,860 --> 01:11:30,530
Hers!

898
01:11:32,840 --> 01:11:35,070
You know the first thing I'm gonna do

899
01:11:35,140 --> 01:11:38,000
after the curse is lifted?

900
01:11:39,580 --> 01:11:43,100
Eat a whole bushel of apples.

901
01:11:50,720 --> 01:11:52,810
Begun by blood.

902
01:11:52,890 --> 01:11:55,620
By blood undone.

903
01:11:59,300 --> 01:12:02,990
Sorry, Jack. I'm not gonna be your leverage.

904
01:12:12,310 --> 01:12:14,540
That's it?

905
01:12:14,610 --> 01:12:16,130
Waste not.

906
01:12:54,220 --> 01:12:55,440
Did it work?

907
01:12:56,290 --> 01:12:59,120
-I don't feel no different. -How do we tell?

908
01:13:05,460 --> 01:13:08,160
You're not dead.

909
01:13:08,230 --> 01:13:09,700
No.

910
01:13:11,000 --> 01:13:11,970
He shot me!

911
01:13:12,030 --> 01:13:13,520
It didn't work.

912
01:13:13,600 --> 01:13:16,260
The curse it still upon us!

913
01:13:20,280 --> 01:13:23,110
You, maid! Your father, what was his name?

914
01:13:23,180 --> 01:13:25,980
Was your father William Turner?

915
01:13:26,050 --> 01:13:27,020
No.

916
01:13:27,080 --> 01:13:30,680
Where's his child that sailed from England eight years ago?

917
01:13:30,750 --> 01:13:34,350
The child in whose veins flows the blood of William Turner?

918
01:13:41,430 --> 01:13:43,460
You two!

919
01:13:43,530 --> 01:13:46,870
You brought us the wrong person!

920
01:13:46,940 --> 01:13:48,230
No!

921
01:13:48,300 --> 01:13:51,100
She had the medallion. She's the proper age.

922
01:13:51,170 --> 01:13:53,640
She said her name was Turner.

923
01:13:55,680 --> 01:13:57,540
I think she lied to us.

924
01:13:59,750 --> 01:14:02,550
You brought us here for nothing.

925
01:14:02,620 --> 01:14:06,250
I won't take questioning or second guesses from you, Twigg.

926
01:14:06,320 --> 01:14:07,550
Who's to blame?

927
01:14:07,620 --> 01:14:11,290
Every decision you've made has led us from bad to worse.

928
01:14:12,290 --> 01:14:15,350
It was you who sent Bootstrap to the depths!

929
01:14:15,430 --> 01:14:17,520
Yeah!

930
01:14:19,270 --> 01:14:22,570
And it's you who brought us here in the first place.

931
01:14:23,640 --> 01:14:27,740
If any coward here dare challenge me, let him speak!

932
01:14:28,510 --> 01:14:32,280
I say we cut her throat and spill all her blood,

933
01:14:32,350 --> 01:14:33,210
just in case.

934
01:14:33,280 --> 01:14:34,770
Yeah!

935
01:14:43,360 --> 01:14:44,590
The medallion!

936
01:14:44,660 --> 01:14:45,990
She's taken it!

937
01:14:46,060 --> 01:14:50,500
Get after her, you feckless pack of ingrates!

938
01:14:54,500 --> 01:14:56,200
No oars here!

939
01:14:56,270 --> 01:14:58,640
Where's the oars?

940
01:14:58,710 --> 01:15:00,900
The oars have gone missing.

941
01:15:00,980 --> 01:15:02,670
Find them!

942
01:15:06,420 --> 01:15:08,580
Find the oars!

943
01:15:11,020 --> 01:15:12,780
You.

944
01:15:13,960 --> 01:15:15,790
You're supposed to be dead!

945
01:15:15,860 --> 01:15:17,660
Am I not?

946
01:15:27,100 --> 01:15:29,800
Palulay.

947
01:15:29,870 --> 01:15:32,270
Palu-li-la-la-lulu.

948
01:15:34,710 --> 01:15:36,510
Parlili.

949
01:15:36,580 --> 01:15:41,640
Parsnip, parsley, partner, partner.

950
01:15:41,720 --> 01:15:42,480
Parley?

951
01:15:42,550 --> 01:15:44,880
That's the one! Parley!

952
01:15:44,950 --> 01:15:46,110
Parley!

953
01:15:46,190 --> 01:15:48,550
Parley?

954
01:15:48,620 --> 01:15:53,560
Damn to the depths whatever man that thought up "parley"!

955
01:15:54,400 --> 01:15:56,260
That would be the French.

956
01:16:07,940 --> 01:16:09,470
Not more pirates.

957
01:16:09,550 --> 01:16:11,910
Welcome aboard, Miss Elizabeth.

958
01:16:12,510 --> 01:16:14,210
Mr. Gibbs?

959
01:16:14,280 --> 01:16:16,580
Hey, boy, where be Jack?

960
01:16:16,650 --> 01:16:18,090
Jack?

961
01:16:18,150 --> 01:16:20,710
Jack Sparrow?

962
01:16:20,790 --> 01:16:22,810
He fell behind.

963
01:16:29,630 --> 01:16:31,190
Keep to the Code.

964
01:16:32,570 --> 01:16:33,870
Weigh anchor!

965
01:16:33,940 --> 01:16:37,060
Hoist the sails! Make quick!

966
01:16:43,080 --> 01:16:46,980
How the blazes did you get off that island?

967
01:16:47,650 --> 01:16:51,050
When you marooned me on that godforsaken spit of land,

968
01:16:51,120 --> 01:16:54,280
you forgot one very important thing, mate.

969
01:16:55,320 --> 01:16:58,320
I'm Captain Jack Sparrow.

970
01:16:58,390 --> 01:17:03,090
Well, I won't be making that mistake again.

971
01:17:03,170 --> 01:17:06,190
Gents, you all remember Captain Jack Sparrow?

972
01:17:06,270 --> 01:17:08,600
Aye.

973
01:17:08,670 --> 01:17:09,800
Kill him.

974
01:17:11,470 --> 01:17:14,440
The girl's blood didn't work, did it?

975
01:17:16,140 --> 01:17:18,270
Hold your fire!

976
01:17:24,120 --> 01:17:27,350
You know whose blood we need.

977
01:17:28,390 --> 01:17:31,150
I know whose blood you need.

978
01:17:37,370 --> 01:17:40,200
What sort of a man trades a man's life for a ship?

979
01:17:40,270 --> 01:17:41,430
Pirate.

980
01:17:42,440 --> 01:17:45,460
Here. Let me.

981
01:17:45,540 --> 01:17:47,200
Thank you.

982
01:17:48,510 --> 01:17:51,880
You said you gave Barbossa my name as yours.

983
01:17:53,180 --> 01:17:53,940
Why?

984
01:17:57,190 --> 01:17:58,580
I don't know.

985
01:18:00,420 --> 01:18:03,360
I'm sorry. Blacksmith's hands. I know they're rough.

986
01:18:03,430 --> 01:18:07,420
No. I mean yes, they are, but...

987
01:18:09,870 --> 01:18:12,800
But don't stop.

988
01:18:20,140 --> 01:18:21,800
Elizabeth.

989
01:18:37,530 --> 01:18:39,260
It's yours.

990
01:18:43,170 --> 01:18:46,660
I thought I'd lost it the day they rescued me.

991
01:18:46,740 --> 01:18:49,170
It was a gift from my father.

992
01:18:49,240 --> 01:18:51,070
He sent it to me.

993
01:18:55,440 --> 01:18:57,540
Why did you take it?

994
01:18:59,550 --> 01:19:03,140
Because I was afraid that you were a pirate.

995
01:19:03,990 --> 01:19:05,850
That would have been awful.

996
01:19:09,190 --> 01:19:11,790
It wasn't your blood they needed.

997
01:19:14,660 --> 01:19:16,560
It was my father's blood.

998
01:19:18,400 --> 01:19:20,030
My blood.

999
01:19:25,940 --> 01:19:27,430
The blood of a pirate.

1000
01:19:27,510 --> 01:19:30,100
Will, I'm so sorry. Please forgive me.

1001
01:19:43,030 --> 01:19:45,050
You expect to leave me on some beach

1002
01:19:45,130 --> 01:19:48,360
with nothing but a name and your word it's the one I need

1003
01:19:48,430 --> 01:19:52,090
and watch you sail away in my ship?

1004
01:19:52,170 --> 01:19:53,160
No.

1005
01:19:53,240 --> 01:19:55,700
I expect to leave you standing on some beach

1006
01:19:55,770 --> 01:19:57,530
with absolutely no name at all,

1007
01:19:57,610 --> 01:20:00,170
watching me sail away on my ship,

1008
01:20:00,240 --> 01:20:02,640
and then I'll shout the name back to you.

1009
01:20:02,710 --> 01:20:04,040
Savvy?

1010
01:20:04,110 --> 01:20:06,770
That still leaves the problem of me on some beach

1011
01:20:06,850 --> 01:20:09,180
with naught but a name and your word

1012
01:20:09,250 --> 01:20:11,740
it's the one I need.

1013
01:20:11,820 --> 01:20:13,620
Of the two of us,

1014
01:20:13,690 --> 01:20:17,320
I'm the only one who hasn't committed mutiny.

1015
01:20:17,390 --> 01:20:21,390
Therefore, my word is the one we'll be trusting.

1016
01:20:21,460 --> 01:20:25,900
Although, I suppose I should be thanking you because, in fact,

1017
01:20:25,970 --> 01:20:29,060
if you hadn't betrayed me and left me to die,

1018
01:20:29,140 --> 01:20:32,800
I would have an equal share in that curse, same as you.

1019
01:20:33,840 --> 01:20:35,740
Funny old world, isn't it?

1020
01:20:39,010 --> 01:20:41,510
Captain, we're coming up on the Interceptor,

1021
01:20:54,630 --> 01:20:57,220
I'm having a thought here, Barbossa.

1022
01:20:57,300 --> 01:20:59,230
What say we run up a flag of truce,

1023
01:20:59,300 --> 01:21:01,130
I scurry over to the Interceptor,

1024
01:21:01,200 --> 01:21:04,970
and I negotiate the return of your medallion?

1025
01:21:05,040 --> 01:21:09,040
Now, that's exactly the attitude that lost you the Pearl,

1026
01:21:09,110 --> 01:21:12,950
People are easy to search when they're dead.

1027
01:21:13,010 --> 01:21:14,810
Lock him in the brig.

1028
01:21:27,100 --> 01:21:29,120
Hands aloft to loose t'gallants!

1029
01:21:29,200 --> 01:21:32,390
With this wind, she'll carry every sail we've got!

1030
01:21:32,470 --> 01:21:33,490
What's happening?

1031
01:21:33,570 --> 01:21:36,970
The Black Pearl, She's gaining on us.

1032
01:21:41,110 --> 01:21:43,010
This is the fastest ship in the Caribbean.

1033
01:21:43,080 --> 01:21:46,140
You can tell them that after they've caught us.

1034
01:21:46,210 --> 01:21:48,010
We're shallower on the draft?

1035
01:21:48,080 --> 01:21:48,910
Aye.

1036
01:21:48,980 --> 01:21:51,920
Well, then can't we lose them amongst those shoals?

1037
01:21:53,050 --> 01:21:55,750
We don't have to outrun them long, just long enough.

1038
01:21:55,820 --> 01:21:58,350
Lighten the ship, stem to stern!

1039
01:21:58,430 --> 01:22:02,160
Anything we can afford to lose, see that it's lost.

1040
01:22:11,140 --> 01:22:13,040
Apparently, there's a leak.

1041
01:22:27,790 --> 01:22:31,160
Haul on the main brace! Make ready the guns!

1042
01:22:31,230 --> 01:22:34,130
And run out the sweeps.

1043
01:22:34,200 --> 01:22:35,960
-Pull! -Ho!

1044
01:23:01,260 --> 01:23:03,420
We're gonna need that.

1045
01:23:11,330 --> 01:23:12,890
It was a good plan.

1046
01:23:12,970 --> 01:23:14,490
Up till now.

1047
01:23:14,570 --> 01:23:15,830
Gibbs!

1048
01:23:15,900 --> 01:23:17,740
We have to make a stand.

1049
01:23:17,810 --> 01:23:19,740
We must fight!

1050
01:23:19,810 --> 01:23:20,970
Load the guns!

1051
01:23:21,040 --> 01:23:22,130
With what?

1052
01:23:22,210 --> 01:23:24,110
Anything. Everything!

1053
01:23:24,180 --> 01:23:26,200
Anything we have left.

1054
01:23:27,580 --> 01:23:29,520
Load the guns!

1055
01:23:29,580 --> 01:23:31,250
Case shot and langrage!

1056
01:23:31,320 --> 01:23:33,690
Nails and crushed glass!

1057
01:23:35,520 --> 01:23:37,420
With a will!

1058
01:23:58,010 --> 01:24:00,740
The Pearl's gonna luff up on our port quarter!

1059
01:24:00,820 --> 01:24:03,550
She'll rake us without ever presenting a target.

1060
01:24:03,620 --> 01:24:05,610
Lower the anchor on the right side.

1061
01:24:06,250 --> 01:24:08,380
On the starboard side!

1062
01:24:08,460 --> 01:24:10,220
It has the element of surprise.

1063
01:24:10,290 --> 01:24:12,820
You're daft, lady! You both are!

1064
01:24:12,890 --> 01:24:14,830
Daft like Jack!

1065
01:24:14,900 --> 01:24:17,130
Lower the starboard anchor!

1066
01:24:17,200 --> 01:24:21,160
Do it, you dogs, or it's you we'll load into the cannons!

1067
01:24:39,150 --> 01:24:41,120
Let go!

1068
01:24:46,930 --> 01:24:49,190
They're clubhauling!

1069
01:24:49,260 --> 01:24:51,530
Hard to port! Rack the starboard oars.

1070
01:24:51,600 --> 01:24:53,830
Hard to port!

1071
01:25:04,780 --> 01:25:07,580
Keep us steady, man.

1072
01:25:13,020 --> 01:25:14,080
Now!

1073
01:25:14,160 --> 01:25:15,180
Fire!

1074
01:25:15,260 --> 01:25:17,420
Fire all!

1075
01:25:25,970 --> 01:25:28,900
Stop blowing holes in my ship!

1076
01:26:14,420 --> 01:26:16,980
We could use a few more ideas, lass.

1077
01:26:17,050 --> 01:26:18,110
Your turn.

1078
01:26:18,190 --> 01:26:20,450
We need us a devil's dowry.

1079
01:26:20,520 --> 01:26:22,750
We'll give them her.

1080
01:26:22,820 --> 01:26:25,050
She's not what they're after.

1081
01:26:26,930 --> 01:26:28,260
The medallion.

1082
01:26:49,520 --> 01:26:52,950
Raise your colors, you bloomin' cockroaches!

1083
01:26:53,020 --> 01:26:55,750
Hands, grapnels, at the ready.

1084
01:26:55,820 --> 01:26:58,160
Prepare to board!

1085
01:27:31,960 --> 01:27:34,330
Blast all to carcasses, men!

1086
01:27:34,400 --> 01:27:36,590
Forward clear to the powder magazine.

1087
01:27:36,660 --> 01:27:41,800
And the rest of you, bring me that medallion!

1088
01:27:49,810 --> 01:27:51,110
Help!

1089
01:27:51,180 --> 01:27:53,170
Hey!

1090
01:28:10,060 --> 01:28:11,730
Below!

1091
01:28:31,150 --> 01:28:32,980
Thanks very much.

1092
01:28:45,670 --> 01:28:47,130
Jack!

1093
01:28:48,000 --> 01:28:49,770
Bloody empty.

1094
01:28:53,640 --> 01:28:54,970
Aaah!

1095
01:29:05,490 --> 01:29:07,720
That's not very nice.

1096
01:29:12,230 --> 01:29:13,420
Where's the medallion?

1097
01:29:13,490 --> 01:29:15,290
Wretch!

1098
01:29:16,230 --> 01:29:17,630
Ahh.

1099
01:29:17,700 --> 01:29:19,670
Where's dear William?

1100
01:29:20,940 --> 01:29:22,400
Will!

1101
01:29:24,240 --> 01:29:25,330
Will!

1102
01:29:25,410 --> 01:29:26,840
Elizabeth!

1103
01:29:30,080 --> 01:29:31,840
Monkey!

1104
01:29:37,620 --> 01:29:39,450
I can't move it!

1105
01:29:39,520 --> 01:29:40,490
Come on!

1106
01:29:40,560 --> 01:29:41,780
Will!

1107
01:29:41,860 --> 01:29:42,980
Elizabeth!

1108
01:29:55,300 --> 01:29:57,360
Why, thank you, Jack.

1109
01:29:57,440 --> 01:29:59,070
You're welcome.

1110
01:29:59,140 --> 01:30:02,840
Not you. We named the monkey "Jack."

1111
01:30:02,910 --> 01:30:07,310
Gents, our hope is restored!

1112
01:30:42,550 --> 01:30:45,880
Any of you so much as thinks the word "parley,"

1113
01:30:45,950 --> 01:30:49,410
I'll have your guts for garters.

1114
01:30:56,400 --> 01:30:57,990
Will!

1115
01:30:58,070 --> 01:31:00,660
You've got to stop it!

1116
01:31:00,740 --> 01:31:02,930
-Stop it! -Welcome back, miss.

1117
01:31:03,000 --> 01:31:05,730
You took advantage of our hospitality last time.

1118
01:31:05,810 --> 01:31:09,470
It holds fair now you return the favor.

1119
01:31:14,920 --> 01:31:16,140
Barbossa!

1120
01:31:18,190 --> 01:31:19,980
Will.

1121
01:31:21,890 --> 01:31:24,080
She goes free.

1122
01:31:24,160 --> 01:31:26,490
What's in your head, boy?

1123
01:31:27,160 --> 01:31:28,420
She goes free!

1124
01:31:28,500 --> 01:31:32,260
You've only got one shot, and we can't die.

1125
01:31:32,330 --> 01:31:34,230
Don't do anything stupid.

1126
01:31:37,310 --> 01:31:38,930
You can't.

1127
01:31:39,010 --> 01:31:40,670
I can.

1128
01:31:41,680 --> 01:31:43,270
Like that.

1129
01:31:44,280 --> 01:31:46,140
Who are you?

1130
01:31:46,210 --> 01:31:47,740
No one. He's no one.

1131
01:31:47,820 --> 01:31:51,270
A distant cousin of my aunt's nephew twice removed.

1132
01:31:51,350 --> 01:31:53,450
Lovely singing voice, though. Eunuch.

1133
01:31:53,520 --> 01:31:55,150
My name is Will Turner.

1134
01:31:55,220 --> 01:31:57,950
My father was Bootstrap Bill Turner.

1135
01:31:58,030 --> 01:32:00,620
His blood runs in my veins.

1136
01:32:00,700 --> 01:32:03,460
He's the spitting image of old Bootstrap Bill

1137
01:32:03,530 --> 01:32:04,860
come back to haunt us.

1138
01:32:04,930 --> 01:32:08,830
On my word, do as I say, or I'll pull this trigger

1139
01:32:08,900 --> 01:32:12,100
and be lost to Davy Jones' locker.

1140
01:32:12,170 --> 01:32:14,470
Name your terms, Mr. Turner.

1141
01:32:14,540 --> 01:32:15,670
Elizabeth goes free.

1142
01:32:15,740 --> 01:32:18,440
Yes, we know that one. Anything else?

1143
01:32:21,280 --> 01:32:24,810
And the crew. The crew are not to be harmed.

1144
01:32:29,320 --> 01:32:30,950
Agreed.

1145
01:32:37,230 --> 01:32:38,720
Go on, poppet, go!

1146
01:32:38,800 --> 01:32:40,360
Walk the plank!

1147
01:32:50,340 --> 01:32:54,040
Barbossa, you lying bastard! You swore she'd go free!

1148
01:32:54,120 --> 01:32:56,880
Don't dare impugn me honor, boy.

1149
01:32:56,950 --> 01:32:58,510
I agreed she'd go free,

1150
01:32:58,590 --> 01:33:02,450
but it was you who failed to specify when or where.

1151
01:33:07,860 --> 01:33:11,560
Though does seem a shame to lose something so fine, don't it?

1152
01:33:11,630 --> 01:33:13,160
Aye.

1153
01:33:13,230 --> 01:33:16,600
So I'll be having that dress back before you go.

1154
01:33:20,810 --> 01:33:22,000
I always liked you.

1155
01:33:26,150 --> 01:33:27,910
Goes with your black heart.

1156
01:33:29,420 --> 01:33:31,850
Ooh, it's still warm.

1157
01:33:34,460 --> 01:33:36,010
Off you go!

1158
01:33:36,620 --> 01:33:38,460
Come on!

1159
01:33:41,630 --> 01:33:43,150
Too long!

1160
01:33:53,940 --> 01:33:56,910
I'd really rather hoped we were past all this.

1161
01:33:57,850 --> 01:34:01,210
Jack...

1162
01:34:02,280 --> 01:34:03,750
Did you not notice?

1163
01:34:03,820 --> 01:34:08,780
That be the same island we made you governor of on our last little trip.

1164
01:34:09,790 --> 01:34:11,050
I did notice.

1165
01:34:11,130 --> 01:34:12,560
Perhaps you'll be able to conjure up

1166
01:34:12,630 --> 01:34:15,260
another miraculous escape.

1167
01:34:15,330 --> 01:34:17,060
But I doubt it.

1168
01:34:19,800 --> 01:34:21,360
Off you go.

1169
01:34:21,440 --> 01:34:22,300
Yeah.

1170
01:34:22,370 --> 01:34:24,960
Last time, you left me a pistol with one shot.

1171
01:34:25,040 --> 01:34:27,060
By the powers, you're right.

1172
01:34:27,140 --> 01:34:30,630
Where be Jack's pistol? Bring it forward.

1173
01:34:33,780 --> 01:34:37,180
Seeing as there's two of us, a gentleman would give us

1174
01:34:37,250 --> 01:34:38,510
a pair of pistols.

1175
01:34:38,590 --> 01:34:40,280
lt'll be one pistol as before,

1176
01:34:40,350 --> 01:34:42,790
You can be the gentleman and shoot the lady

1177
01:34:42,860 --> 01:34:45,790
and starve to death yourself.

1178
01:35:09,150 --> 01:35:10,240
That's the second time

1179
01:35:10,320 --> 01:35:13,770
I've had to watch that man sail away with my ship.

1180
01:35:15,520 --> 01:35:18,110
But you were marooned on this island before.

1181
01:35:18,190 --> 01:35:19,780
We can escape the same way you did.

1182
01:35:19,860 --> 01:35:22,720
To what point and purpose, young missy?

1183
01:35:22,790 --> 01:35:24,690
The Black Pearl is gone.

1184
01:35:24,760 --> 01:35:28,320
Unless you have a rudder and sails hidden in that bodice.

1185
01:35:28,400 --> 01:35:30,590
Unlikely.

1186
01:35:30,670 --> 01:35:34,200
Young Mr. Turner will be dead long before you can reach him.

1187
01:35:36,510 --> 01:35:38,500
But you're Captain Jack Sparrow.

1188
01:35:38,580 --> 01:35:42,480
You vanished from seven agents of the East lndia Company.

1189
01:35:42,550 --> 01:35:45,570
You sacked Nassau Port without even firing a shot.

1190
01:35:45,650 --> 01:35:48,710
Are you the pirate I've read about or not?

1191
01:35:50,590 --> 01:35:52,560
How did you escape last time?

1192
01:35:59,260 --> 01:36:03,700
Last time, I was here a grand total of three days, all right?

1193
01:36:04,900 --> 01:36:06,430
Last time...

1194
01:36:07,940 --> 01:36:10,910
The rumrunners used this island as a cache,

1195
01:36:10,980 --> 01:36:13,940
came by, and I was able to barter passage off.

1196
01:36:14,010 --> 01:36:15,340
From the looks of things,

1197
01:36:15,410 --> 01:36:17,750
they've long been out of business.

1198
01:36:17,820 --> 01:36:22,310
Probably have your bloody friend Norrington to thank for that.

1199
01:36:22,920 --> 01:36:25,680
So that's it, then?

1200
01:36:25,760 --> 01:36:27,820
That's the secret grand adventure

1201
01:36:27,890 --> 01:36:30,420
of the infamous Jack Sparrow?

1202
01:36:30,500 --> 01:36:35,490
You spent three days lying on a beach, drinking rum?

1203
01:36:37,670 --> 01:36:40,030
Welcome to the Caribbean, love.

1204
01:36:48,980 --> 01:36:50,570
BOTH:  We're devils, we're black sheep

1205
01:36:50,650 --> 01:36:52,170
We're really bad eggs

1206
01:36:52,250 --> 01:36:54,510
Drink up, me hearties, yo ho

1207
01:36:54,590 --> 01:36:56,450
Yo ho, yo ho

1208
01:36:56,520 --> 01:36:57,450
Ouch!

1209
01:36:57,520 --> 01:36:58,890
A pirate's life for me

1210
01:36:58,960 --> 01:37:00,950
I love this song!

1211
01:37:03,960 --> 01:37:06,990
Really bad eggs!

1212
01:37:07,070 --> 01:37:08,050
Ooh.

1213
01:37:11,000 --> 01:37:12,870
When I get the Pearl back,

1214
01:37:12,940 --> 01:37:15,600
I'm gonna teach it to the whole crew.

1215
01:37:15,670 --> 01:37:17,230
We'll sing it all the time.

1216
01:37:17,310 --> 01:37:21,180
You'll be the most fearsome pirate in the Spanish Main.

1217
01:37:21,250 --> 01:37:24,040
Not just the Spanish Main, love.

1218
01:37:24,120 --> 01:37:25,670
The entire ocean.

1219
01:37:25,750 --> 01:37:28,410
The entire world.

1220
01:37:28,490 --> 01:37:32,080
Wherever we want to go, we go. That's what a ship is, you know.

1221
01:37:32,160 --> 01:37:34,990
It's not just a keel and a deck and sails.

1222
01:37:35,060 --> 01:37:36,550
That's what a ship needs.

1223
01:37:36,630 --> 01:37:44,330
But what a ship is, what the Black Pearl really is,

1224
01:37:44,400 --> 01:37:46,170
is freedom.

1225
01:37:48,510 --> 01:37:52,640
Jack, it must be really terrible for you

1226
01:37:52,710 --> 01:37:55,180
to be trapped on this island.

1227
01:37:55,250 --> 01:37:56,580
Oh, yes.

1228
01:37:56,650 --> 01:38:01,020
But the company is infinitely better than last time,

1229
01:38:01,090 --> 01:38:02,310
I think.

1230
01:38:04,020 --> 01:38:06,250
The scenery has definitely improved.

1231
01:38:06,320 --> 01:38:07,810
-Mr. Sparrow. -Mm-hmm?

1232
01:38:07,890 --> 01:38:10,420
I'm not entirely sure I've had enough rum

1233
01:38:10,500 --> 01:38:12,660
to allow that kind of talk.

1234
01:38:13,600 --> 01:38:16,190
I know exactly what you mean, love.

1235
01:38:22,870 --> 01:38:24,430
To freedom.

1236
01:38:27,040 --> 01:38:29,140
To the Black Pearl,

1237
01:38:58,440 --> 01:39:00,810
No! Not good!

1238
01:39:00,880 --> 01:39:03,470
Stop! Not good!

1239
01:39:03,550 --> 01:39:04,670
What are you doing?

1240
01:39:04,750 --> 01:39:07,340
You burned all the food, the shade! The rum!

1241
01:39:07,420 --> 01:39:09,080
Yes, the rum is gone.

1242
01:39:09,150 --> 01:39:10,880
Why is the rum gone?

1243
01:39:10,960 --> 01:39:12,720
One, because it is a vile drink

1244
01:39:12,790 --> 01:39:15,620
that turns even the most respectable men into scoundrels.

1245
01:39:15,690 --> 01:39:18,790
Two, that signal is over a thousand feet high.

1246
01:39:18,860 --> 01:39:21,090
The entire Royal Navy is looking for me.

1247
01:39:21,170 --> 01:39:24,790
Do you think there is the slightest chance they won't see?

1248
01:39:24,870 --> 01:39:26,700
But why is the rum gone?

1249
01:39:27,670 --> 01:39:29,700
Just wait, Captain Sparrow.

1250
01:39:29,770 --> 01:39:32,540
You give it one hour, maybe two, and you will see

1251
01:39:32,610 --> 01:39:35,010
white sails on that horizon.

1252
01:39:41,850 --> 01:39:45,750
"Must've been terrible for you to be trapped here, Jack."

1253
01:39:45,820 --> 01:39:48,220
Well, it bloody is now!

1254
01:39:55,130 --> 01:39:57,730
There'll be no living with her after this.

1255
01:39:58,440 --> 01:40:00,400
-But we've got to save Will. -No.

1256
01:40:00,470 --> 01:40:02,230
You're safe now.

1257
01:40:02,310 --> 01:40:04,000
We will return to Port Royal,

1258
01:40:04,080 --> 01:40:06,040
not go gallivanting after pirates!

1259
01:40:06,110 --> 01:40:08,080
Then we condemn him to death.

1260
01:40:09,280 --> 01:40:12,080
The boy's fate is regrettable,

1261
01:40:12,150 --> 01:40:14,670
but so was his decision to engage in piracy.

1262
01:40:14,750 --> 01:40:18,450
To rescue me. To prevent anything from happening to me.

1263
01:40:18,520 --> 01:40:22,010
If I may be so bold as to inject my professional opinion.

1264
01:40:22,090 --> 01:40:25,090
The Pearl was listing near to scuppers after the battle.

1265
01:40:25,160 --> 01:40:28,100
It's very unlikely she'll be able to make good time.

1266
01:40:28,170 --> 01:40:30,000
Think about it.

1267
01:40:30,070 --> 01:40:32,000
The Black Pearl,

1268
01:40:32,070 --> 01:40:36,340
The last real pirate threat in the Caribbean, mate.

1269
01:40:36,410 --> 01:40:38,880
How can you pass that up, right?

1270
01:40:40,510 --> 01:40:43,310
By remembering that I serve others, Mr. Sparrow.

1271
01:40:43,380 --> 01:40:44,710
Not only myself.

1272
01:40:44,780 --> 01:40:47,010
Commodore, I beg you, please do this.

1273
01:40:47,080 --> 01:40:48,520
For me.

1274
01:40:48,590 --> 01:40:50,880
As a wedding gift.

1275
01:40:50,960 --> 01:40:53,080
Elizabeth.

1276
01:40:53,160 --> 01:40:56,420
Are you accepting the commodore's proposal?

1277
01:40:58,460 --> 01:40:59,660
I am.

1278
01:40:59,730 --> 01:41:01,560
A wedding! I love weddings!

1279
01:41:01,630 --> 01:41:03,530
Drinks all around!

1280
01:41:05,770 --> 01:41:06,900
I know.

1281
01:41:06,970 --> 01:41:09,300
"Clap him in irons," right?

1282
01:41:11,140 --> 01:41:15,340
Mr. Sparrow, you will accompany these fine men to the helm

1283
01:41:15,410 --> 01:41:18,580
and provide us with a bearing to lsla de Muerta.

1284
01:41:18,650 --> 01:41:20,240
You will spend the rest of the voyage

1285
01:41:20,320 --> 01:41:22,750
contemplating all meanings of the phrase

1286
01:41:22,820 --> 01:41:25,020
"silent as the grave."

1287
01:41:25,090 --> 01:41:26,650
Do I make myself clear?

1288
01:41:26,720 --> 01:41:29,320
lnescapably clear.

1289
01:41:32,830 --> 01:41:36,030
Shiver me timbers.

1290
01:41:36,100 --> 01:41:38,160
Cotton here says you missed a bit.

1291
01:41:42,540 --> 01:41:44,570
You knew William Turner?

1292
01:41:45,640 --> 01:41:48,130
Ol' Bootstrap Bill.

1293
01:41:48,210 --> 01:41:50,010
We knew him.

1294
01:41:50,080 --> 01:41:54,040
Never sat well with Bootstrap, what we did to Jack Sparrow.

1295
01:41:54,120 --> 01:41:56,640
The mutiny and all.

1296
01:41:56,720 --> 01:41:59,550
He said it wasn't right with the Code.

1297
01:41:59,620 --> 01:42:03,580
That's why he sent off a piece of the treasure to you.

1298
01:42:04,400 --> 01:42:08,060
He said we deserved to be cursed.

1299
01:42:08,130 --> 01:42:10,190
And remain cursed.

1300
01:42:10,270 --> 01:42:11,290
Stupid blighter.

1301
01:42:11,370 --> 01:42:13,200
Good man.

1302
01:42:13,770 --> 01:42:15,860
Well, as you can imagine,

1303
01:42:15,940 --> 01:42:18,770
that didn't sit too well with the captain.

1304
01:42:18,840 --> 01:42:22,070
That didn't sit too well with the captain at all.

1305
01:42:22,150 --> 01:42:26,280
-Tell him what Barbossa did. -I'm telling the story!

1306
01:42:28,120 --> 01:42:31,990
So, what the captain did.

1307
01:42:32,060 --> 01:42:36,350
He strapped a cannon to Bootstrap's bootstraps.

1308
01:42:36,430 --> 01:42:39,490
And last we saw of Ol' Bill Turner,

1309
01:42:39,560 --> 01:42:44,930
he was sinking to the crushing, black oblivion of Davy Jones' locker.

1310
01:42:45,870 --> 01:42:47,570
'Course it was only after that

1311
01:42:47,640 --> 01:42:50,570
we learned we needed his blood to lift the curse.

1312
01:42:50,640 --> 01:42:53,040
That's what you call ironic.

1313
01:42:57,950 --> 01:42:59,250
Bring him.

1314
01:43:05,860 --> 01:43:07,980
I don't care for the situation.

1315
01:43:08,060 --> 01:43:11,620
Any attempt to storm the caves could turn to an ambush.

1316
01:43:11,690 --> 01:43:15,030
Not if you're the one doing the ambushing.

1317
01:43:15,100 --> 01:43:19,230
I convince Barbossa to send his men out with their little boats.

1318
01:43:19,300 --> 01:43:21,530
You and your mates return to the Dauntless

1319
01:43:21,600 --> 01:43:24,970
and blast the bejesus out of them with your little cannons.

1320
01:43:25,040 --> 01:43:27,270
What do you have to lose?

1321
01:43:27,340 --> 01:43:30,610
Nothing I'd lament being rid of.

1322
01:43:31,450 --> 01:43:34,080
Now, to be quite honest with you,

1323
01:43:34,150 --> 01:43:36,080
there's a risk to those aboard the Dauntless,

1324
01:43:36,150 --> 01:43:39,640
which includes the future Mrs. Commodore.

1325
01:43:39,720 --> 01:43:41,880
Sorry, but it's for your own safety.

1326
01:43:41,960 --> 01:43:44,320
The commodore ordered! I have to tell him!

1327
01:43:44,390 --> 01:43:45,620
The pirates!

1328
01:43:45,700 --> 01:43:47,600
They're cursed! They can't be killed!

1329
01:43:47,660 --> 01:43:49,830
Don't worry. He's informed of that.

1330
01:43:49,900 --> 01:43:54,060
A mermaid flopped up on deck and told him the whole story.

1331
01:43:54,140 --> 01:43:56,870
This is Jack Sparrow's doing!

1332
01:44:07,550 --> 01:44:08,920
No reason to fret.

1333
01:44:08,990 --> 01:44:11,950
Just a prick of the finger. A few drops of blood.

1334
01:44:12,020 --> 01:44:13,650
No mistakes this time.

1335
01:44:13,720 --> 01:44:17,720
He's only half Turner. We spill it all!

1336
01:44:19,530 --> 01:44:21,390
Guess there is reason to fret.

1337
01:44:35,280 --> 01:44:37,340
Beg your pardon. Beg your pardon.

1338
01:44:37,410 --> 01:44:39,240
-Begun by blood. -Excuse me.

1339
01:44:39,320 --> 01:44:42,080
By blood --

1340
01:44:44,720 --> 01:44:46,150
Jack!

1341
01:44:46,420 --> 01:44:47,550
It's not possible.

1342
01:44:47,620 --> 01:44:49,750
Not probable.

1343
01:44:50,360 --> 01:44:52,830
Where's Elizabeth?

1344
01:44:52,900 --> 01:44:55,390
She's safe, just like I promised.

1345
01:44:55,470 --> 01:44:58,230
She's set to marry Norrington, like she promised.

1346
01:44:58,300 --> 01:45:00,630
You get to die for her, like you promised.

1347
01:45:00,700 --> 01:45:03,370
So we're all men of our word, really.

1348
01:45:03,440 --> 01:45:06,240
Except for Elizabeth who is, in fact, a woman.

1349
01:45:06,310 --> 01:45:08,740
Shut up! You're next.

1350
01:45:10,750 --> 01:45:13,940
You don't want to be doing that, mate.

1351
01:45:14,020 --> 01:45:16,750
No, I really think I do.

1352
01:45:17,750 --> 01:45:19,780
Your funeral.

1353
01:45:22,990 --> 01:45:25,460
Why don't I want to be doing it?

1354
01:45:25,530 --> 01:45:27,360
Well, because...

1355
01:45:27,430 --> 01:45:32,530
Because the HMS Dauntless, pride of the Royal Navy,

1356
01:45:32,600 --> 01:45:37,770
is floating just offshore, waiting for you.

1357
01:45:45,780 --> 01:45:47,180
What we doing here?

1358
01:45:48,720 --> 01:45:52,750
The pirates come out, unprepared and unawares.

1359
01:45:52,820 --> 01:45:58,420
We catch them in a crossfire, send them down to see Old Hob.

1360
01:45:58,490 --> 01:46:00,190
I know why we're here.

1361
01:46:00,260 --> 01:46:01,920
I meant, why aren't we doing

1362
01:46:02,000 --> 01:46:05,870
what Mr. Sparrow said we should do with the cannons and all?

1363
01:46:05,940 --> 01:46:09,560
Because it was Mr. Sparrow who said it.

1364
01:46:12,210 --> 01:46:14,730
You think he wasn't telling the truth?

1365
01:46:14,810 --> 01:46:17,010
Just hear me out, mate.

1366
01:46:17,080 --> 01:46:19,240
You order your men to row to the Dauntless,

1367
01:46:19,320 --> 01:46:21,440
They do what they do best.

1368
01:46:21,520 --> 01:46:23,510
Robert's your uncle. Fannie's your aunt.

1369
01:46:23,590 --> 01:46:26,280
There you are with two ships.

1370
01:46:26,360 --> 01:46:28,550
The makings of your very own fleet.

1371
01:46:28,620 --> 01:46:32,220
You'll take the grandest as your flagship, and who's to argue?

1372
01:46:32,300 --> 01:46:34,260
But what of the Pearl?

1373
01:46:35,400 --> 01:46:37,160
Name me captain.

1374
01:46:37,230 --> 01:46:40,170
I'll sail under your colors. I'll give you 10% of me plunder.

1375
01:46:40,240 --> 01:46:46,000
And you get to introduce yourself as Commodore Barbossa.

1376
01:46:46,080 --> 01:46:47,010
Savvy?

1377
01:46:47,080 --> 01:46:50,710
I suppose in exchange, you want me not to kill the whelp.

1378
01:46:50,780 --> 01:46:53,870
No, no, no. Not at all. By all means, kill the whelp.

1379
01:46:53,950 --> 01:46:56,250
Just not yet.

1380
01:46:56,320 --> 01:47:01,990
Wait to lift the curse until the opportune moment.

1381
01:47:04,160 --> 01:47:06,720
For instance...

1382
01:47:08,030 --> 01:47:11,470
After you've killed Norrington's men.

1383
01:47:11,530 --> 01:47:16,400
Every last one.

1384
01:47:26,050 --> 01:47:28,570
You've been planning this from the beginning.

1385
01:47:28,650 --> 01:47:30,640
Ever since you learned my name.

1386
01:47:30,720 --> 01:47:31,810
Yeah.

1387
01:47:34,120 --> 01:47:35,890
I want 50% of your plunder.

1388
01:47:35,960 --> 01:47:37,390
-15%.  -40%.

1389
01:47:37,460 --> 01:47:39,490
25%\n25%.

1390
01:47:39,560 --> 01:47:40,890
I'll buy you the hat.

1391
01:47:40,960 --> 01:47:44,900
A really big one, Commodore.

1392
01:47:47,740 --> 01:47:50,600
We have an accord.

1393
01:47:50,670 --> 01:47:53,230
All hands to the boats!

1394
01:47:54,210 --> 01:47:55,340
Apologies.

1395
01:47:55,410 --> 01:47:57,110
You give the orders.

1396
01:47:58,280 --> 01:48:02,450
Gents, take a walk.

1397
01:48:07,020 --> 01:48:08,920
Not to the boats?

1398
01:49:07,120 --> 01:49:09,110
Hold fire.

1399
01:49:12,160 --> 01:49:16,350
Oh, this is just like what the Greeks done at Troy.

1400
01:49:16,430 --> 01:49:20,260
Except they was in a horse instead of dresses.

1401
01:49:20,330 --> 01:49:21,730
Wooden horse.

1402
01:49:34,580 --> 01:49:36,240
A moment, please.

1403
01:49:39,820 --> 01:49:41,210
Elizabeth?

1404
01:49:44,050 --> 01:49:46,820
I just want you to know

1405
01:49:46,890 --> 01:49:49,880
I believe you made a very good decision today.

1406
01:49:49,960 --> 01:49:52,090
Couldn't be more proud of you.

1407
01:49:53,100 --> 01:49:55,290
But, you know, even a good decision,

1408
01:49:55,360 --> 01:49:59,320
if made for the wrong reasons, can be a wrong decision.

1409
01:50:05,840 --> 01:50:07,500
Look into that. Report back.

1410
01:50:07,580 --> 01:50:08,810
Lieutenant?

1411
01:50:15,520 --> 01:50:17,990
Yoo-hoo!

1412
01:50:27,800 --> 01:50:29,200
Elizabeth?

1413
01:50:31,030 --> 01:50:32,520
Are you there?

1414
01:50:34,600 --> 01:50:37,230
Elizabeth, are you even listening to me?

1415
01:51:03,500 --> 01:51:06,430
Oh, what have you done?

1416
01:51:06,500 --> 01:51:08,200
-Oooh! -Stop that!

1417
01:51:08,270 --> 01:51:10,900
Already feel like a fool.

1418
01:51:10,970 --> 01:51:12,700
Look nice, though.

1419
01:51:16,850 --> 01:51:19,080
I look nice?

1420
01:51:55,220 --> 01:51:58,580
I must admit, Jack, I thought I had you figured.

1421
01:51:58,650 --> 01:52:01,710
But it turns out you're a hard man to predict.

1422
01:52:01,790 --> 01:52:03,730
Me? I'm dishonest.

1423
01:52:03,790 --> 01:52:07,020
And a dishonest man you can always trust to be dishonest.

1424
01:52:07,100 --> 01:52:08,290
Honestly.

1425
01:52:08,360 --> 01:52:10,830
It's the honest ones you want to watch for,

1426
01:52:10,900 --> 01:52:13,200
because you can never predict

1427
01:52:13,270 --> 01:52:17,430
when they're going to do something incredibly stupid.

1428
01:52:43,500 --> 01:52:44,800
Aaah!

1429
01:52:50,570 --> 01:52:52,940
You're off the edge of the map, mate.

1430
01:52:53,010 --> 01:52:55,980
Here there be monsters.

1431
01:52:59,880 --> 01:53:02,740
Right!Right!

1432
01:53:02,820 --> 01:53:04,790
What would you pick to eat first?

1433
01:53:04,850 --> 01:53:06,910
I think we should decide now.

1434
01:53:06,990 --> 01:53:09,960
Just so we're ready when the time comes.

1435
01:53:34,380 --> 01:53:36,080
What was that?

1436
01:53:54,300 --> 01:53:55,400
Shh!

1437
01:53:58,410 --> 01:54:00,100
Miss Elizabeth!

1438
01:54:19,500 --> 01:54:22,400
Make for the ship! Move!

1439
01:54:31,110 --> 01:54:32,900
To the ship!

1440
01:54:35,710 --> 01:54:37,680
Row, men!

1441
01:55:15,150 --> 01:55:17,950
You can't beat me, Jack.

1442
01:55:41,310 --> 01:55:43,540
That's interesting.

1443
01:55:53,660 --> 01:55:55,620
I couldn't resist, mate.

1444
01:56:43,840 --> 01:56:45,640
Aaah!

1445
01:57:02,020 --> 01:57:03,180
Sorry!

1446
01:57:12,270 --> 01:57:13,790
So what now, Jack Sparrow?

1447
01:57:13,870 --> 01:57:16,630
Are we to be two immortals locked in an epic battle

1448
01:57:16,710 --> 01:57:20,160
until judgment day and trumpets sound?

1449
01:57:20,240 --> 01:57:22,330
Or you could surrender.

1450
01:57:30,490 --> 01:57:33,390
Aaaah!

1451
01:57:48,740 --> 01:57:50,100
All of you with me!

1452
01:57:50,170 --> 01:57:52,230
Will is in that cave. We must save him.

1453
01:57:52,310 --> 01:57:55,540
Ready. And heave!

1454
01:58:01,050 --> 01:58:03,580
Please, I need your help! Come on!

1455
01:58:03,650 --> 01:58:06,140
Any port in a storm.

1456
01:58:06,220 --> 01:58:08,750
Cotton's right. We've got the Pearl,

1457
01:58:08,820 --> 01:58:10,880
What about Jack? Are you gonna leave him?

1458
01:58:10,960 --> 01:58:12,790
Jack owes us a ship.

1459
01:58:12,860 --> 01:58:14,890
There's the Code to consider.

1460
01:58:14,960 --> 01:58:16,290
The Code?

1461
01:58:17,830 --> 01:58:19,270
You're pirates.

1462
01:58:19,330 --> 01:58:22,030
Hang the Code, and hang the rules!

1463
01:58:22,100 --> 01:58:25,270
They're more like guidelines anyway.

1464
01:58:27,880 --> 01:58:30,470
Bloody pirates!

1465
01:58:39,960 --> 01:58:41,890
-Hey. -What?

1466
01:58:41,960 --> 01:58:43,930
Is it supposed to be doing that?

1467
01:58:43,990 --> 01:58:46,830
They're stealing our ship!

1468
01:58:46,900 --> 01:58:49,230
Bloody pirates!

1469
01:58:50,570 --> 01:58:53,130
Boarders, away!

1470
01:59:00,680 --> 01:59:02,910
Come on!

1471
01:59:02,980 --> 01:59:04,380
Gaaah!

1472
01:59:28,570 --> 01:59:29,730
Me eye!

1473
01:59:40,120 --> 01:59:42,020
-Aaaah! -Aaaah!

1474
01:59:49,790 --> 01:59:51,920
I'm gonna teach you the meaning of pain!

1475
01:59:51,990 --> 01:59:53,550
Do you like pain?

1476
01:59:55,260 --> 01:59:57,490
Try wearing a corset.

1477
02:00:07,140 --> 02:00:09,440
Whose side is Jack on?

1478
02:00:09,510 --> 02:00:10,980
At the moment?

1479
02:00:25,390 --> 02:00:27,360
Aaaaaah!

1480
02:00:46,820 --> 02:00:47,940
No fair.

1481
02:01:11,640 --> 02:01:17,140
Ten years you carry that pistol, and now you waste your shot.

1482
02:01:17,210 --> 02:01:19,040
He didn't waste it.

1483
02:01:44,870 --> 02:01:46,670
I feel...

1484
02:01:50,910 --> 02:01:52,380
Cold.

1485
02:02:54,480 --> 02:02:56,000
Parley?

1486
02:02:57,050 --> 02:02:58,980
The ship is ours, gentlemen.

1487
02:02:59,050 --> 02:03:00,380
Huzzah!

1488
02:03:00,450 --> 02:03:01,380
Huzzah!

1489
02:03:47,200 --> 02:03:49,530
We should return to the Dauntless,

1490
02:03:52,030 --> 02:03:55,130
Your fiance will be wanting to know you're safe.

1491
02:04:05,380 --> 02:04:08,370
If you were waiting for the opportune moment...

1492
02:04:09,480 --> 02:04:10,970
That was it.

1493
02:04:12,550 --> 02:04:13,680
If you'd be so kind,

1494
02:04:13,760 --> 02:04:16,990
I'd be much obliged if you'd drop me off at my ship.

1495
02:04:22,030 --> 02:04:24,290
I'm sorry, Jack.

1496
02:04:26,230 --> 02:04:31,040
They done what's right by them. Can't expect more than that.

1497
02:04:45,850 --> 02:04:48,910
"Jack Sparrow, be it known that you..."

1498
02:04:48,990 --> 02:04:51,420
Captain. Captain Jack Sparrow.

1499
02:04:51,490 --> 02:04:56,090
"For your willful commission of crimes against the crown."

1500
02:04:56,400 --> 02:04:59,030
"Said crimes being numerous in quantity

1501
02:04:59,100 --> 02:05:01,470
and sinister in nature.

1502
02:05:01,540 --> 02:05:05,270
The most egregious of these to be cited herewith.

1503
02:05:05,340 --> 02:05:07,830
Piracy, smuggling..."

1504
02:05:07,910 --> 02:05:10,210
This is wrong.

1505
02:05:10,280 --> 02:05:13,040
Commodore Norrington is bound by the law.

1506
02:05:13,120 --> 02:05:15,640
As are we all.

1507
02:05:15,720 --> 02:05:18,380
"lmpersonating an officer of the Spanish Royal Navy.

1508
02:05:18,450 --> 02:05:21,550
lmpersonating a cleric of the church of England."

1509
02:05:21,620 --> 02:05:22,780
Ah, yeah.

1510
02:05:22,860 --> 02:05:26,560
"Sailing under false colors, arson, kidnapping,

1511
02:05:26,630 --> 02:05:33,660
looting, poaching, brigandage, pilfering, depravity,

1512
02:05:33,740 --> 02:05:37,640
depredation, and general lawlessness.

1513
02:05:37,710 --> 02:05:40,040
And for these crimes

1514
02:05:40,110 --> 02:05:42,670
you have been sentenced to be, on this day,

1515
02:05:42,740 --> 02:05:46,440
hung by the neck until dead.

1516
02:05:46,520 --> 02:05:48,880
May God have mercy on your soul."

1517
02:05:48,950 --> 02:05:51,320
Governor Swann.

1518
02:05:51,390 --> 02:05:53,120
Commodore.

1519
02:05:54,020 --> 02:05:55,610
Elizabeth.

1520
02:05:56,390 --> 02:05:59,880
I should have told you every day from the moment I met you.

1521
02:06:02,330 --> 02:06:03,490
I love you.

1522
02:06:20,450 --> 02:06:21,510
Marines.

1523
02:06:21,580 --> 02:06:23,310
I can't breathe.

1524
02:06:24,520 --> 02:06:26,180
Elizabeth!

1525
02:06:28,490 --> 02:06:29,550
Move!

1526
02:06:41,370 --> 02:06:42,630
What...

1527
02:06:53,950 --> 02:06:55,540
Move!

1528
02:07:14,540 --> 02:07:15,970
Come on!

1529
02:07:16,040 --> 02:07:18,160
-Right! -Come on, lads!

1530
02:07:43,270 --> 02:07:48,130
I thought we might endure some ill-conceived escape attempt.

1531
02:07:48,200 --> 02:07:49,670
But not from you.

1532
02:07:49,740 --> 02:07:53,540
On our return to Port Royal, I granted you clemency.

1533
02:07:53,610 --> 02:07:55,270
And this is how you thank me?

1534
02:07:55,340 --> 02:07:58,040
By throwing in your lot with him?

1535
02:07:58,110 --> 02:07:59,050
He's a pirate!

1536
02:07:59,110 --> 02:08:01,110
And a good man.

1537
02:08:02,420 --> 02:08:04,320
If all I have achieved is the hangman

1538
02:08:04,390 --> 02:08:08,050
will earn two pairs of boots instead of one, so be it.

1539
02:08:08,120 --> 02:08:09,890
My conscience will be clear.

1540
02:08:09,960 --> 02:08:13,720
You forget your place, Turner.

1541
02:08:14,630 --> 02:08:18,360
It's right here between you and Jack.

1542
02:08:22,400 --> 02:08:23,390
As is mine.

1543
02:08:23,470 --> 02:08:24,800
Elizabeth!

1544
02:08:26,170 --> 02:08:28,440
Lower your weapons.

1545
02:08:28,510 --> 02:08:30,940
For goodness sake, put them down!

1546
02:08:35,350 --> 02:08:38,950
So this is where your heart truly lies, then?

1547
02:08:39,820 --> 02:08:41,380
It is.

1548
02:08:49,900 --> 02:08:53,660
Well! I'm actually feeling rather good about this.

1549
02:08:53,740 --> 02:08:56,260
I think we've all arrived at a special place.

1550
02:08:56,340 --> 02:08:57,400
Spiritually.

1551
02:08:57,470 --> 02:08:59,570
Ecumenically.

1552
02:08:59,640 --> 02:09:01,670
Grammatically.

1553
02:09:02,340 --> 02:09:06,010
I want you to know that I was rooting for you, mate.

1554
02:09:06,080 --> 02:09:07,450
Know that.

1555
02:09:11,390 --> 02:09:12,680
Elizabeth.

1556
02:09:14,660 --> 02:09:17,960
It would never have worked between us, darling.

1557
02:09:19,290 --> 02:09:20,590
I'm sorry.

1558
02:09:22,900 --> 02:09:23,690
Will.

1559
02:09:26,200 --> 02:09:27,630
Nice hat.

1560
02:09:29,100 --> 02:09:31,200
Friends.

1561
02:09:33,340 --> 02:09:36,440
This is a day that you will always remember

1562
02:09:36,510 --> 02:09:37,880
as the day that...

1563
02:09:43,350 --> 02:09:46,950
ldiot. He has nowhere to go but back to the noose.

1564
02:09:48,120 --> 02:09:50,460
Sail ho!

1565
02:09:58,030 --> 02:10:00,660
What's your plan of action?

1566
02:10:01,300 --> 02:10:02,600
Sir?

1567
02:10:06,880 --> 02:10:09,140
Perhaps on the rare occasion

1568
02:10:09,210 --> 02:10:13,240
pursuing the right course demands an act of piracy,

1569
02:10:13,310 --> 02:10:17,340
piracy itself can be the right course?

1570
02:10:21,390 --> 02:10:23,320
Mr. Turner.

1571
02:10:28,700 --> 02:10:31,490
I will accept the consequences of my actions.

1572
02:10:37,410 --> 02:10:39,530
This is a beautiful sword.

1573
02:10:41,680 --> 02:10:45,170
I would expect the man who made it to show the same care

1574
02:10:45,250 --> 02:10:48,180
in every aspect of his life.

1575
02:10:50,190 --> 02:10:51,950
Thank you.

1576
02:10:54,590 --> 02:10:56,520
Commodore!

1577
02:10:56,590 --> 02:10:58,350
What about Sparrow?

1578
02:10:59,530 --> 02:11:03,220
Well, I think we can afford to give him one day's head start.

1579
02:11:08,970 --> 02:11:09,800
So.

1580
02:11:11,240 --> 02:11:14,830
This is the path you've chosen, is it?

1581
02:11:16,180 --> 02:11:21,080
After all, he is a blacksmith.

1582
02:11:21,150 --> 02:11:22,510
No.

1583
02:11:27,260 --> 02:11:29,310
He's a pirate.

1584
02:11:54,220 --> 02:11:55,940
Heave!

1585
02:12:05,790 --> 02:12:08,730
Thought you were supposed to keep to the Code.

1586
02:12:08,800 --> 02:12:12,930
We figured they were more actual guidelines.

1587
02:12:17,040 --> 02:12:18,800
Thank you.

1588
02:12:19,810 --> 02:12:21,710
Captain Sparrow.

1589
02:12:25,510 --> 02:12:27,710
The Black Pearl is yours.

1590
02:12:42,900 --> 02:12:44,630
On deck, you scabrous dogs!

1591
02:12:44,700 --> 02:12:46,430
Hands to braces!

1592
02:12:46,500 --> 02:12:48,970
Let down and haul to run free!

1593
02:12:50,340 --> 02:12:54,640
Now, bring me that horizon.

1594
02:13:01,180 --> 02:13:04,050
And really bad eggs.

1595
02:13:06,920 --> 02:13:09,150
Drink up, me hearties, yo ho!

